{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1792429933868,"sessionID":"ses_eaada65b7ffeHiWlwBKhGghsv7","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_01_fODbAyzN3jKzganwmKif2161","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/148-session-relaunch-deep-review/.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-research/references/state/state-jsonl.md"},"output":"<path>/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/148-session-relaunch-deep-review/.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-research/references/state/state-jsonl.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: ---\n2: title: Deep Research JSONL State Reference\n3: description: Config, iteration, event, lineage, graph, and blocked-stop records for deep-research state logs.\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"research state jsonl\"\n6:   - \"research config record\"\n7:   - \"research iteration records\"\n8:   - \"research negative knowledge\"\n9:   - \"research graph events\"\n10:   - \"research event records\"\n11: importance_tier: normal\n12: contextType: implementation\n13: version: 1.14.0.4\n14: ---\n15: \n16: # Deep Research JSONL State Reference\n17: \n18: `deep-research-state.jsonl` is append-only. Each line is one JSON object. The first line is normally the config record, followed by iteration and event records.\n19: \n20: ---\n21: \n22: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n23: \n24: ### Purpose\n25: \n26: Define the append-only JSONL records used by deep-research config, iteration, lifecycle, graph, pause, stuck-recovery, and blocked-stop state.\n27: \n28: ### When to Use\n29: \n30: Load this reference when validating state logs, adding event records, reconstructing a packet, or mapping legacy stop labels to current values.\n31: \n32: ### Core Principle\n33: \n34: Raw JSONL is append-only evidence. Reducer-owned files derive from it, but they do not replace it.\n35: \n36: ### How Records Get Written\n37: \n38: Canonical records are written by calling the append gateway, not by writing to the file:\n39: \n40: ```bash\n41: node .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/append-mode-event.cjs \\\n42:   --mode research \\\n43:   --run-directory <spec folder> \\\n44:   --event-json <path to one JSON record>\n45: ```\n46: \n47: The gateway authorizes the write against the mode's durable authority record, serialises it behind\n48: the ledger fence, returns a receipt, and refreshes this file from the ledger. Exit `0` means the\n49: record is durable; exit `2` means it was refused and the refusal names the check that failed.\n50: \n51: Writing to `deep-research-state.jsonl` directly bypasses all four of those properties. Two writers\n52: with no shared ordering can interleave, and a record nobody authorized carries no receipt, so a\n53: later reader cannot tell an accepted record from an invented one. The file below remains the\n54: readable surface — six consumers depend on it — but under ledger authority it is a projection of the\n55: ledger rather than the place writes land.\n56: \n57: That split is authority-dependent: while the mode's authority record still names the legacy writer\n58: (`authority-deep-research.json`, state `legacy_authoritative`), this file is the authoritative target\n59: and no ledger backs it; the projection relationship begins when the record moves to\n60: `new_authoritative_reversible` or `new_authoritative_final` with `selectedWriter: \"dark\"`. The\n61: registered stems and their producer census live in `state-format.md` section 7, and\n62: `.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/check-ledger-stem-producers.cjs` fails when the\n63: census and the emitter surface on disk disagree.\n64: \n65: ---\n66: \n67: ## 2. CONFIG RECORD\n68: \n69: The config record captures the initialized loop contract.\n70: \n71: ```json\n72: {\n73:   \"type\": \"config\",\n74:   \"topic\": \"API response time optimization\",\n75:   \"maxIterations\": 10,\n76:   \"convergenceThreshold\": 0.05,\n77:   \"stuckThreshold\": 3,\n78:   \"specFolder\": \"028-auto-deep-research\",\n79:   \"createdAt\": \"2026-05-24T00:00:00Z\"\n80: }\n81: ```\n82: \n83: The full config file also stores executor, lineage, reducer, capability matrix, pause sentinel, archive, and file-protection settings.\n84: \n85: A ledger projection rebuilds this row thin — only `type`, `topic`, `maxIterations`, `generation`, and `timestamp` — so a refresh that would replace the row while dropping keys from an existing `type: \"config\"` first line is refused (`ATTRIBUTION_COLLAPSE`) rather than published. Move or migrate the legacy row deliberately before cutting the mode over.\n86: \n87: ---\n88: \n89: ## 3. ITERATION RECORDS\n90: \n91: ```json\n92: {\n93:   \"type\": \"iteration\",\n94:   \"iteration\": 1,\n95:   \"status\": \"complete\",\n96:   \"focus\": \"Initial broad survey\",\n97:   \"findingsCount\": 5,\n98:   \"newInfoRatio\": 1.0,\n99:   \"noveltyJustification\": \"First pass; all findings are new to this packet\",\n100:   \"keyQuestions\": [\"What causes latency?\"],\n101:   \"answeredQuestions\": [\"What causes latency?\"],\n102:   \"timestamp\": \"2026-05-24T00:05:00Z\",\n103:   \"durationMs\": 45000\n104: }\n105: ```\n106: \n107: | Field | Required | Notes |\n108: |-------|----------|-------|\n109: | `type` | Yes | `iteration` |\n110: | `iteration` | Yes | 1-indexed iteration number. The fan-out stop-policy validator reads only this field |\n111: | `run` | Legacy | Older name for `iteration`. Readers still accept it; new records write `iteration` |\n112: | `status` | Yes | See status values below |\n113: | `focus` | Yes | Single focus investigated |\n114: | `findingsCount` | Yes | Number of findings reported |\n115: | `newInfoRatio` | Yes | Novelty score used by convergence |\n116: | `noveltyJustification` | Recommended | One-sentence explanation of the ratio |\n117: | `keyQuestions` | Recommended | Questions considered during this iteration |\n118: | `answeredQuestions` | Recommended | Questions answered by evidence |\n119: | `ruledOut` | Optional | Negative knowledge entries |\n120: | `graphEvents` | Optional | Coverage graph mutations |\n121: \n122: Event records in §7 keep their own `run` field, which names the iteration the event occurred in.\n123: \n124: Status values:\n125: \n126: | Status | Meaning |\n127: |--------|---------|\n128: | `complete` | Normal evidence-gathering iteration |\n129: | `timeout` | Time/tool budget exceeded |\n130: | `error` | Tool, parse, or unexpected failure |\n131: | `stuck` | No new information and no clear next direction |\n132: | `insight` | Low ratio but important conceptual breakthrough |\n133: | `thought` | Analytical-only iteration; excluded from stuck and signal math |\n134: \n135: ---\n136: \n137: ## 4. CONVERGENCE SIGNAL FIELDS\n138: \n139: When the composite algorithm runs, the iteration may include signal values:\n140: \n141: ```json\n142: {\n143:   \"type\": \"iteration\",\n144:   \"iteration\": 5,\n145:   \"convergenceSignals\": {\n146:     \"rollingAvg\": 0.12,\n147:     \"madScore\": 0.08,\n148:     \"entropyCoverage\": 0.71,\n149:     \"compositeStop\": 0.42\n150:   }\n151: }\n152: ```\n153: \n154: Signal rules live in `../convergence/convergence-signals.md`.\n155: \n156: ---\n157: \n158: ## 5. NEGATIVE KNOWLEDGE\n159: \n160: Use `ruledOut` to preserve eliminated approaches.\n161: \n162: ```json\n163: {\n164:   \"type\": \"iteration\",\n165:   \"iteration\": 3,\n166:   \"status\": \"complete\",\n167:   \"ruledOut\": [\n168:     {\n169:       \"approach\": \"HTTP/3 multiplexing\",\n170:       \"reason\": \"No server-side support in target environment\",\n171:       \"evidence\": \"file:docs/protocols.md:42\"\n172:     }\n173:   ]\n174: }\n175: ```\n176: \n177: Ruled-out directions should also appear in strategy and synthesis outputs.\n178: \n179: ---\n180: \n181: ## 6. GRAPH EVENTS\n182: \n183: `graphEvents` records coverage graph mutations from an iteration.\n184: \n185: ```json\n186: {\n187:   \"type\": \"iteration\",\n188:   \"iteration\": 4,\n189:   \"graphEvents\": [\n190:     {\"type\": \"question\", \"id\": \"q-reconnect\", \"label\": \"How should reconnect work?\"},\n191:     {\"type\": \"finding\", \"id\": \"f-backoff\", \"label\": \"Exponential backoff is required\"},\n192:     {\"type\": \"edge\", \"from\": \"f-backoff\", \"to\": \"q-reconnect\", \"relation\": \"ANSWERS\"}\n193:   ]\n194: }\n195: ```\n196: \n197: Valid node types are `question`, `finding`, `claim`, and `source`; `edge` records connect nodes. IDs must be unique within a single `(specFolder, loopType, sessionId)` namespace. Reusing the same logical ID across independent sessions is allowed because the coverage graph uses a composite key.\n198: \n199: ---\n200: \n201: ## 7. EVENT RECORDS\n202: \n203: Lifecycle event:\n204: \n205: ```json\n206: {\n207:   \"type\": \"event\",\n208:   \"event\": \"resumed\",\n209:   \"mode\": \"research\",\n210:   \"sessionId\": \"dr-2026-05-24T00-00-00Z\",\n211:   \"parentSessionId\": \"dr-2026-05-24T00-00-00Z\",\n212:   \"lineageMode\": \"resume\",\n213:   \"continuedFromRun\": 4,\n214:   \"generation\": 1,\n215:   \"archivedPath\": null,\n216:   \"timestamp\": \"2026-05-24T00:00:00Z\"\n217: }\n218: ```\n219: \n220: Blocked stop event:\n221: \n222: ```json\n223: {\n224:   \"type\": \"event\",\n225:   \"event\": \"blocked_stop\",\n226:   \"mode\": \"research\",\n227:   \"run\": 7,\n228:   \"stopReason\": \"blockedStop\",\n229:   \"blockedBy\": [\"keyQuestionCoverage\", \"sourceDiversity\"],\n230:   \"recoveryStrategy\": \"focus_pivot\",\n231:   \"timestamp\": \"2026-05-24T00:00:00Z\"\n232: }\n233: ```\n234: \n235: Pause event:\n236: \n237: ```json\n238: {\n239:   \"type\": \"event\",\n240:   \"event\": \"userPaused\",\n241:   \"mode\": \"research\",\n242:   \"run\": 7,\n243:   \"stopReason\": \"userPaused\",\n244:   \"sentinelPath\": \"research/.deep-research-pause\",\n245:   \"timestamp\": \"2026-05-24T00:00:00Z\"\n246: }\n247: ```\n248: \n249: Stuck recovery event:\n250: \n251: ```json\n252: {\n253:   \"type\": \"event\",\n254:   \"event\": \"stuckRecovery\",\n255:   \"mode\": \"research\",\n256:   \"run\": 7,\n257:   \"stopReason\": \"stuckRecovery\",\n258:   \"fromFocus\": \"Connection pooling\",\n259:   \"toFocus\": \"Transport alternatives\",\n260:   \"strategy\": \"try_opposites\",\n261:   \"timestamp\": \"2026-05-24T00:00:00Z\"\n262: }\n263: ```\n264: \n265: Idea observation event:\n266: \n267: ```json\n268: {\n269:   \"type\": \"event\",\n270:   \"event\": \"idea_observed\",\n271:   \"mode\": \"research\",\n272:   \"run\": 7,\n273:   \"ideaId\": \"idea-cache-stampede\",\n274:   \"idea\": \"Investigate cache stampede mitigation as a follow-up focus\",\n275:   \"category\": \"ideas\",\n276:   \"source\": \"iteration-007.md\",\n277:   \"timestamp\": \"2026-05-24T00:00:00Z\"\n278: }\n279: ```\n280: \n281: Leaf agents may record `idea_observed` through the gateway for promising tangents. They must not record `idea_promoted`; promotion is reducer-owned.\n282: \n283: Idea promotion event:\n284: \n285: ```json\n286: {\n287:   \"type\": \"event\",\n288:   \"event\": \"idea_promoted\",\n289:   \"mode\": \"research\",\n290:   \"run\": 8,\n291:   \"ideaId\": \"idea-cache-stampede\",\n292:   \"idea\": \"Investigate cache stampede mitigation as a follow-up focus\",\n293:   \"category\": \"ideas\",\n294:   \"observationCount\": 2,\n295:   \"minIdeaObservations\": 2,\n296:   \"firstObservedRun\": 6,\n297:   \"lastObservedRun\": 7,\n298:   \"timestamp\": \"2026-05-24T00:02:00Z\"\n299: }\n300: ```\n301: \n302: The reducer emits `idea_promoted` once per idea after replay shows `observationCount >= minIdeaObservations`. `minIdeaObservations` defaults to `2` and is clamped to the inclusive range `1..10`.\n303: \n304: Rejected idea event:\n305: \n306: ```json\n307: {\n308:   \"type\": \"event\",\n309:   \"event\": \"idea_rejected\",\n310:   \"mode\": \"research\",\n311:   \"run\": 7,\n312:   \"ideaId\": \"idea-cache-stampede\",\n313:   \"idea\": \"Investigate cache stampede mitigation as a follow-up focus\",\n314:   \"category\": \"ideas\",\n315:   \"reason\": \"Already rejected by environment evidence\",\n316:   \"timestamp\": \"2026-05-24T00:00:00Z\"\n317: }\n318: ```\n319: \n320: `idea_rejected` durably suppresses the matching promoted idea and also enters the reducer-owned rejected-pattern cache. Use `category: \"general\"` only when the rejection should suppress matching text across next-focus, recovery, and ideas candidates.\n321: \n322: Rejected idea removal event:\n323: \n324: ```json\n325: {\n326:   \"type\": \"event\",\n327:   \"event\": \"ideaRejectedRemoved\",\n328:   \"mode\": \"research\",\n329:   \"pattern\": \"Retry HTTP/3 as the primary latency fix\",\n330:   \"category\": \"next-focus\",\n331:   \"timestamp\": \"2026-05-24T00:00:00Z\"\n332: }\n333: ```\n334: \n335: Rejected idea reset event:\n336: \n337: ```json\n338: {\n339:   \"type\": \"event\",\n340:   \"event\": \"ideaRejectedReset\",\n341:   \"mode\": \"research\",\n342:   \"reason\": \"Operator wants previously rejected ideas eligible again\",\n343:   \"timestamp\": \"2026-05-24T00:00:00Z\"\n344: }\n345: ```\n346: \n347: The reducer derives a bounded active rejected-pattern cache from these events. `idea_rejected` adds or refreshes one pattern, `ideaRejectedRemoved` removes a single matching pattern or id, and `ideaRejectedReset` clears the active cache. Legacy `ideaRejected`, `ideaRejectedRemoved`, and `ideaRejectedReset` rows remain replayable for existing state logs. The active cache is capped at 100 entries; when more are added, the oldest active entry is evicted and the reducer emits a warning.\n348: \n349: Candidate checks compare normalized exact text first, then apply fuzzy matching only when the candidate category is compatible with the rejected category. Omit `category` for a general rejection that can suppress candidates across next-focus, recovery, or ideas surfaces.\n350: \n351: Graph convergence event shape lives in `../convergence/convergence-graph.md`.\n352: \n353: ---\n354: \n355: ## 8. NORMALIZATION\n356: \n357: Emission-time normalization maps legacy labels into canonical `stopReason` values before persistence. The mapping lives in `../convergence/convergence.md`.\n358: \n359: Malformed lines are handled by the reducer according to its strict/lenient mode. Fault tolerance and reconstruction are documented in `state-reducer-registry.md`.\n\n(End of file - total 359 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"---\ntitle: Deep Research JSONL State Reference\ndescription: Config, iteration, event, lineage, graph, and blocked-stop records for deep-research state logs.\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"research state jsonl\"\n  - \"research config record\"\n  - \"research iteration records\"\n  - \"research negative knowledge\"\n  - \"research graph events\"\n  - \"research event records\"\nimportance_tier: normal\ncontextType: implementation\nversion: 1.14.0.4\n---\n\n# Deep Research JSONL State Reference\n\n`deep-research-state.jsonl` is append-only. Each line is one JSON object. The first line is normally the config record, followed by iteration and event records.\n\n---","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/148-session-relaunch-deep-review/.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-research/references/state/state-jsonl.md","text":"---\ntitle: Deep Research JSONL State Reference\ndescription: Config, iteration, event, lineage, graph, and blocked-stop records for deep-research state logs.\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"research state jsonl\"\n  - \"research config record\"\n  - \"research iteration records\"\n  - \"research negative knowledge\"\n  - \"research graph events\"\n  - \"research event records\"\nimportance_tier: normal\ncontextType: implementation\nversion: 1.14.0.4\n---\n\n# Deep Research JSONL State Reference\n\n`deep-research-state.jsonl` is append-only. Each line is one JSON object. The first line is normally the config record, followed by iteration and event records.\n\n---\n\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\n### Purpose\n\nDefine the append-only JSONL records used by deep-research config, iteration, lifecycle, graph, pause, stuck-recovery, and blocked-stop state.\n\n### When to Use\n\nLoad this reference when validating state logs, adding event records, reconstructing a packet, or mapping legacy stop labels to current values.\n\n### Core Principle\n\nRaw JSONL is append-only evidence. Reducer-owned files derive from it, but they do not replace it.\n\n### How Records Get Written\n\nCanonical records are written by calling the append gateway, not by writing to the file:\n\n```bash\nnode .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/append-mode-event.cjs \\\n  --mode research \\\n  --run-directory <spec folder> \\\n  --event-json <path to one JSON record>\n```\n\nThe gateway authorizes the write against the mode's durable authority record, serialises it behind\nthe ledger fence, returns a receipt, and refreshes this file from the ledger. Exit `0` means the\nrecord is durable; exit `2` means it was refused and the refusal names the check that failed.\n\nWriting to `deep-research-state.jsonl` directly bypasses all four of those properties. Two writers\nwith no shared ordering can interleave, and a record nobody authorized carries no receipt, so a\nlater reader cannot tell an accepted record from an invented one. The file below remains the\nreadable surface — six consumers depend on it — but under ledger authority it is a projection of the\nledger rather than the place writes land.\n\nThat split is authority-dependent: while the mode's authority record still names the legacy writer\n(`authority-deep-research.json`, state `legacy_authoritative`), this file is the authoritative target\nand no ledger backs it; the projection relationship begins when the record moves to\n`new_authoritative_reversible` or `new_authoritative_final` with `selectedWriter: \"dark\"`. The\nregistered stems and their producer census live in `state-format.md` section 7, and\n`.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/check-ledger-stem-producers.cjs` fails when the\ncensus and the emitter surface on disk disagree.\n\n---\n\n## 2. CONFIG RECORD\n\nThe config record captures the initialized loop contract.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"type\": \"config\",\n  \"topic\": \"API response time optimization\",\n  \"maxIterations\": 10,\n  \"convergenceThreshold\": 0.05,\n  \"stuckThreshold\": 3,\n  \"specFolder\": \"028-auto-deep-research\",\n  \"createdAt\": \"2026-05-24T00:00:00Z\"\n}\n```\n\nThe full config file also stores executor, lineage, reducer, capability matrix, pause sentinel, archive, and file-protection settings.\n\nA ledger projection rebuilds this row thin — only `type`, `topic`, `maxIterations`, `generation`, and `timestamp` — so a refresh that would replace the row while dropping keys from an existing `type: \"config\"` first line is refused (`ATTRIBUTION_COLLAPSE`) rather than published. Move or migrate the legacy row deliberately before cutting the mode over.\n\n---\n\n## 3. ITERATION RECORDS\n\n```json\n{\n  \"type\": \"iteration\",\n  \"iteration\": 1,\n  \"status\": \"complete\",\n  \"focus\": \"Initial broad survey\",\n  \"findingsCount\": 5,\n  \"newInfoRatio\": 1.0,\n  \"noveltyJustification\": \"First pass; all findings are new to this packet\",\n  \"keyQuestions\": [\"What causes latency?\"],\n  \"answeredQuestions\": [\"What causes latency?\"],\n  \"timestamp\": \"2026-05-24T00:05:00Z\",\n  \"durationMs\": 45000\n}\n```\n\n| Field | Required | Notes |\n|-------|----------|-------|\n| `type` | Yes | `iteration` |\n| `iteration` | Yes | 1-indexed iteration number. The fan-out stop-policy validator reads only this field |\n| `run` | Legacy | Older name for `iteration`. Readers still accept it; new records write `iteration` |\n| `status` | Yes | See status values below |\n| `focus` | Yes | Single focus investigated |\n| `findingsCount` | Yes | Number of findings reported |\n| `newInfoRatio` | Yes | Novelty score used by convergence |\n| `noveltyJustification` | Recommended | One-sentence explanation of the ratio |\n| `keyQuestions` | Recommended | Questions considered during this iteration |\n| `answeredQuestions` | Recommended | Questions answered by evidence |\n| `ruledOut` | Optional | Negative knowledge entries |\n| `graphEvents` | Optional | Coverage graph mutations |\n\nEvent records in §7 keep their own `run` field, which names the iteration the event occurred in.\n\nStatus values:\n\n| Status | Meaning |\n|--------|---------|\n| `complete` | Normal evidence-gathering iteration |\n| `timeout` | Time/tool budget exceeded |\n| `error` | Tool, parse, or unexpected failure |\n| `stuck` | No new information and no clear next direction |\n| `insight` | Low ratio but important conceptual breakthrough |\n| `thought` | Analytical-only iteration; excluded from stuck and signal math |\n\n---\n\n## 4. CONVERGENCE SIGNAL FIELDS\n\nWhen the composite algorithm runs, the iteration may include signal values:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"type\": \"iteration\",\n  \"iteration\": 5,\n  \"convergenceSignals\": {\n    \"rollingAvg\": 0.12,\n    \"madScore\": 0.08,\n    \"entropyCoverage\": 0.71,\n    \"compositeStop\": 0.42\n  }\n}\n```\n\nSignal rules live in `../convergence/convergence-signals.md`.\n\n---\n\n## 5. NEGATIVE KNOWLEDGE\n\nUse `ruledOut` to preserve eliminated approaches.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"type\": \"iteration\",\n  \"iteration\": 3,\n  \"status\": \"complete\",\n  \"ruledOut\": [\n    {\n      \"approach\": \"HTTP/3 multiplexing\",\n      \"reason\": \"No server-side support in target environment\",\n      \"evidence\": \"file:docs/protocols.md:42\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nRuled-out directions should also appear in strategy and synthesis outputs.\n\n---\n\n## 6. GRAPH EVENTS\n\n`graphEvents` records coverage graph mutations from an iteration.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"type\": \"iteration\",\n  \"iteration\": 4,\n  \"graphEvents\": [\n    {\"type\": \"question\", \"id\": \"q-reconnect\", \"label\": \"How should reconnect work?\"},\n    {\"type\": \"finding\", \"id\": \"f-backoff\", \"label\": \"Exponential backoff is required\"},\n    {\"type\": \"edge\", \"from\": \"f-backoff\", \"to\": \"q-reconnect\", \"relation\": \"ANSWERS\"}\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nValid node types are `question`, `finding`, `claim`, and `source`; `edge` records connect nodes. IDs must be unique within a single `(specFolder, loopType, sessionId)` namespace. Reusing the same logical ID across independent sessions is allowed because the coverage graph uses a composite key.\n\n---\n\n## 7. EVENT RECORDS\n\nLifecycle event:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"type\": \"event\",\n  \"event\": \"resumed\",\n  \"mode\": \"research\",\n  \"sessionId\": \"dr-2026-05-24T00-00-00Z\",\n  \"parentSessionId\": \"dr-2026-05-24T00-00-00Z\",\n  \"lineageMode\": \"resume\",\n  \"continuedFromRun\": 4,\n  \"generation\": 1,\n  \"archivedPath\": null,\n  \"timestamp\": \"2026-05-24T00:00:00Z\"\n}\n```\n\nBlocked stop event:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"type\": \"event\",\n  \"event\": \"blocked_stop\",\n  \"mode\": \"research\",\n  \"run\": 7,\n  \"stopReason\": \"blockedStop\",\n  \"blockedBy\": [\"keyQuestionCoverage\", \"sourceDiversity\"],\n  \"recoveryStrategy\": \"focus_pivot\",\n  \"timestamp\": \"2026-05-24T00:00:00Z\"\n}\n```\n\nPause event:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"type\": \"event\",\n  \"event\": \"userPaused\",\n  \"mode\": \"research\",\n  \"run\": 7,\n  \"stopReason\": \"userPaused\",\n  \"sentinelPath\": \"research/.deep-research-pause\",\n  \"timestamp\": \"2026-05-24T00:00:00Z\"\n}\n```\n\nStuck recovery event:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"type\": \"event\",\n  \"event\": \"stuckRecovery\",\n  \"mode\": \"research\",\n  \"run\": 7,\n  \"stopReason\": \"stuckRecovery\",\n  \"fromFocus\": \"Connection pooling\",\n  \"toFocus\": \"Transport alternatives\",\n  \"strategy\": \"try_opposites\",\n  \"timestamp\": \"2026-05-24T00:00:00Z\"\n}\n```\n\nIdea observation event:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"type\": \"event\",\n  \"event\": \"idea_observed\",\n  \"mode\": \"research\",\n  \"run\": 7,\n  \"ideaId\": \"idea-cache-stampede\",\n  \"idea\": \"Investigate cache stampede mitigation as a follow-up focus\",\n  \"category\": \"ideas\",\n  \"source\": \"iteration-007.md\",\n  \"timestamp\": \"2026-05-24T00:00:00Z\"\n}\n```\n\nLeaf agents may record `idea_observed` through the gateway for promising tangents. They must not record `idea_promoted`; promotion is reducer-owned.\n\nIdea promotion event:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"type\": \"event\",\n  \"event\": \"idea_promoted\",\n  \"mode\": \"research\",\n  \"run\": 8,\n  \"ideaId\": \"idea-cache-stampede\",\n  \"idea\": \"Investigate cache stampede mitigation as a follow-up focus\",\n  \"category\": \"ideas\",\n  \"observationCount\": 2,\n  \"minIdeaObservations\": 2,\n  \"firstObservedRun\": 6,\n  \"lastObservedRun\": 7,\n  \"timestamp\": \"2026-05-24T00:02:00Z\"\n}\n```\n\nThe reducer emits `idea_promoted` once per idea after replay shows `observationCount >= minIdeaObservations`. `minIdeaObservations` defaults to `2` and is clamped to the inclusive range `1..10`.\n\nRejected idea event:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"type\": \"event\",\n  \"event\": \"idea_rejected\",\n  \"mode\": \"research\",\n  \"run\": 7,\n  \"ideaId\": \"idea-cache-stampede\",\n  \"idea\": \"Investigate cache stampede mitigation as a follow-up focus\",\n  \"category\": \"ideas\",\n  \"reason\": \"Already rejected by environment evidence\",\n  \"timestamp\": \"2026-05-24T00:00:00Z\"\n}\n```\n\n`idea_rejected` durably suppresses the matching promoted idea and also enters the reducer-owned rejected-pattern cache. Use `category: \"general\"` only when the rejection should suppress matching text across next-focus, recovery, and ideas candidates.\n\nRejected idea removal event:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"type\": \"event\",\n  \"event\": \"ideaRejectedRemoved\",\n  \"mode\": \"research\",\n  \"pattern\": \"Retry HTTP/3 as the primary latency fix\",\n  \"category\": \"next-focus\",\n  \"timestamp\": \"2026-05-24T00:00:00Z\"\n}\n```\n\nRejected idea reset event:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"type\": \"event\",\n  \"event\": \"ideaRejectedReset\",\n  \"mode\": \"research\",\n  \"reason\": \"Operator wants previously rejected ideas eligible again\",\n  \"timestamp\": \"2026-05-24T00:00:00Z\"\n}\n```\n\nThe reducer derives a bounded active rejected-pattern cache from these events. `idea_rejected` adds or refreshes one pattern, `ideaRejectedRemoved` removes a single matching pattern or id, and `ideaRejectedReset` clears the active cache. Legacy `ideaRejected`, `ideaRejectedRemoved`, and `ideaRejectedReset` rows remain replayable for existing state logs. The active cache is capped at 100 entries; when more are added, the oldest active entry is evicted and the reducer emits a warning.\n\nCandidate checks compare normalized exact text first, then apply fuzzy matching only when the candidate category is compatible with the rejected category. Omit `category` for a general rejection that can suppress candidates across next-focus, recovery, or ideas surfaces.\n\nGraph convergence event shape lives in `../convergence/convergence-graph.md`.\n\n---\n\n## 8. NORMALIZATION\n\nEmission-time normalization maps legacy labels into canonical `stopReason` values before persistence. The mapping lives in `../convergence/convergence.md`.\n\nMalformed lines are handled by the reducer according to its strict/lenient mode. Fault tolerance and reconstruction are documented in `state-reducer-registry.md`.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":359,"totalLines":359,"truncated":false}},"title":".skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-research/references/state/state-jsonl.md","time":{"start":1792429933862,"end":1792429933867}},"id":"prt_155264454001DPA0IXRJJ27qMO","sessionID":"ses_eaada65b7ffeHiWlwBKhGghsv7","messageID":"msg_155262dfc001lyjDMObbcIeY1d"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1792429934264,"sessionID":"ses_eaada65b7ffeHiWlwBKhGghsv7","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_00_xpyeqquQkBAgX6gxT35v8150","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"node .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/append-mode-event.cjs --help 2>&1 | head -60"},"output":"{\"ok\":false,\"phase\":\"runtime\",\"reason\":\"mode is required\",\"code\":\"RUNTIME_ERROR\"}\n","metadata":{"output":"{\"ok\":false,\"phase\":\"runtime\",\"reason\":\"mode is required\",\"code\":\"RUNTIME_ERROR\"}\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"node .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/append-mode-event.cjs --help 2>&1 | head -60","time":{"start":1792429933634,"end":1792429934263}},"id":"prt_1552643c0001V3uJ1sDn2h7Gy6","sessionID":"ses_eaada65b7ffeHiWlwBKhGghsv7","messageID":"msg_155262dfc001lyjDMObbcIeY1d"}}
