{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1792430060770,"sessionID":"ses_eaada65b7ffeHiWlwBKhGghsv7","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_00_BhiYLv3CbY12FU6AbHVB4263","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"ls .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/dist/continuity/generate-context.js 2>&1; grep -n \"stdin\\|full-auto\" .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/src/continuity/generate-context.ts 2>/dev/null | head -20; echo \"---DIST---\"; grep -n \"stdin\\|full-auto\" .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/dist/continuity/generate-context.js 2>/dev/null | head -20"},"output":".skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/dist/continuity/generate-context.js\n---DIST---\n44:  --stdin           Read structured JSON from stdin (preferred when a caller already has curated session data)\n47:  --planner-mode <mode>  plan-only (default), hybrid or full-auto. Every mode refreshes\n49:                         only full-auto writes the continuity fields. hybrid behaves like plan-only.\n50:  --full-auto       Shortcut for --planner-mode full-auto\n56:  echo '{\"specFolder\":\"specs/001-feature/\"}' | node generate-context.js --stdin\n67:  phase-parent pointers. Only --full-auto also writes the continuity fields\n71:  - ALWAYS use --stdin, --json, or a JSON temp file.\n122:  Continuity fields (all optional, written only by a --full-auto save):\n718:async function readAllStdin(stdin = process.stdin) {\n721:        stdin.setEncoding('utf8');\n722:        stdin.on('data', (chunk) => {\n725:        stdin.on('end', () => resolve(buffer));\n726:        stdin.on('error', reject);\n746:async function parseStructuredModeArguments(mode, args, stdinReader) {\n747:    if (mode === '--stdin' && process.stdin.isTTY) {\n748:        console.error('--stdin requires piped input');\n751:    const rawJson = mode === '--stdin'\n752:        ? await stdinReader()\n763:    const explicitSpecFolderArg = args[mode === '--stdin' ? 1 : 2];\n785:async function parseArguments(argv = process.argv.slice(2), stdinReader = readAllStdin) {\n","metadata":{"output":".skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/dist/continuity/generate-context.js\n---DIST---\n44:  --stdin           Read structured JSON from stdin (preferred when a caller already has curated session data)\n47:  --planner-mode <mode>  plan-only (default), hybrid or full-auto. Every mode refreshes\n49:                         only full-auto writes the continuity fields. hybrid behaves like plan-only.\n50:  --full-auto       Shortcut for --planner-mode full-auto\n56:  echo '{\"specFolder\":\"specs/001-feature/\"}' | node generate-context.js --stdin\n67:  phase-parent pointers. Only --full-auto also writes the continuity fields\n71:  - ALWAYS use --stdin, --json, or a JSON temp file.\n122:  Continuity fields (all optional, written only by a --full-auto save):\n718:async function readAllStdin(stdin = process.stdin) {\n721:        stdin.setEncoding('utf8');\n722:        stdin.on('data', (chunk) => {\n725:        stdin.on('end', () => resolve(buffer));\n726:        stdin.on('error', reject);\n746:async function parseStructuredModeArguments(mode, args, stdinReader) {\n747:    if (mode === '--stdin' && process.stdin.isTTY) {\n748:        console.error('--stdin requires piped input');\n751:    const rawJson = mode === '--stdin'\n752:        ? await stdinReader()\n763:    const explicitSpecFolderArg = args[mode === '--stdin' ? 1 : 2];\n785:async function parseArguments(argv = process.argv.slice(2), stdinReader = readAllStdin) {\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"ls .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/dist/continuity/generate-context.js 2>&1; grep -n \"stdin\\|full-auto\" .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/src/continuity/generate-context.ts 2>/dev/null | head -20; echo \"---DIST---\"; grep -n \"stdin\\|full-auto\" .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/dist/continuity/generate-context.js 2>/dev/null | head -20","time":{"start":1792430060738,"end":1792430060769}},"id":"prt_15528336e001Cx1StRL1pAjphS","sessionID":"ses_eaada65b7ffeHiWlwBKhGghsv7","messageID":"msg_155280402001FDnfykZgiRk61z"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1792430060949,"sessionID":"ses_eaada65b7ffeHiWlwBKhGghsv7","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_01_qMVHKZv8lACL0PTftOs64372","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"grep -n \"exit 3\\|exit code\\|--stdin\\|full-auto\\|hop limit\" .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/README.md | head -40"},"output":"65:The wrapper exports one loop id and starts `SKILLED_LOOP_HOPS` at 0. It relaunches only when a matching record is armed, fresh and below the configured hop limit.\n73:The nudge reports the fill as `[session-relaunch] Context is about N% full.`, or as `[session-relaunch] Context is past the configured window (over 100% full).` when the ratio is above 1. It then lets the session finish instead: `If the remaining work fits in a few more steps, finish it instead and skip these steps.` A session with more work left does the handoff steps now, before any other work, so a fresh session continues before auto-compaction has to. The nudge gives the operator the last word. If the operator has told the session not to hand off, it skips the note, continuity save and prepare steps, keeps working and leaves auto-compaction as the fallback. The nudge names a handoff note under `.skilled/skills/.state/session-relaunch/handoffs/` and a prepare command. The path resolves under the repository that holds the concern, so a runtime started in a subdirectory still writes under the repo root. The note needs flat frontmatter and four non-empty sections: Goal, Done, Remaining work and In-flight state. The Remaining work section lists only unfinished task work. Note creation, continuity saving and `prepare` are handoff steps. When `spec_folder` is a packet path, Claude uses `/speckit:save`. Other runtimes send the save JSON to the [continuity writer](../../../.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/dist/continuity/generate-context.js) with `--stdin --full-auto <spec-folder>` before prepare. `spec_folder` holds the repo-relative packet path, `none` when Gate 3 was answered D) Skip or empty when Gate 3 was never answered.\n85:Pi checks usage at `message_end` and holds the nudge for `agent_end`, because the message that crosses the threshold usually carries the prepare call itself. At `agent_end` it re-checks the pending or fresh armed record and the hop limit, then sends the held text with `deliverAs: 'followUp'` and `triggerTurn: true`, so the nudge reaches the model without an operator turn. Its adapter finds `killer.cjs` by walking up from any repository subdirectory. `tsconfig.pi.json` includes the extension entry so the adapter is type-checked. OpenCode adds its active nudge to the system prompt on every request while this session has no pending or fresh armed record, and outside `skilled-loop` while the planned note file does not exist. It keeps a claim seed in the system prompt until the note leaves `open` or a new non-seed user message arrives, then arms the record on `session.idle`.\n103:The killer lock stores its owner pid, a creation timestamp and an expiry timestamp. The expiry is the idle timeout plus 5000 ms of signal wait, 3000 ms of notification time and a 60000 ms margin. A lock past its expiry, or a two-field lock from an earlier build that is older than 10 minutes, does not count as a live owner even while its pid exists, because that pid may by then belong to an unrelated process. A live owner or a lock younger than five seconds without a readable owner pid logs `killer-lock-held`. Taking over a stale lock first acquires a takeover mutex, the `<lock>.takeover` directory, whose `owner` file records the pid of the killer that holds it. The mutex is reclaimed only when that owner is dead. A mutex with no owner file is reclaimed once it is older than 30 s. The reclaim is an atomic rename. A live holder keeps exclusion only while the directory stays younger than the 10-minute hold bound, because a real holder holds the mutex for a re-check, an unlink and a create, never minutes. Past that bound the recorded pid counts as reused and the mutex is reclaimed. A killer that cannot acquire the mutex logs `killer-lock-held`. The mutex holder re-checks the lock, logs `killer-lock-stale`, removes the lock and retries once. Leftover `.stale-` takeover directories older than 30 s are swept under the mutex. The killer records the inode of the mutex directory it creates and, on release, removes the path only while that inode still matches, so it never removes a takeover directory it did not create. The killer releases the lock file the same way: it removes the file only while the recorded owner pid is its own, and logs `killer-lock-not-owned` when a contender has taken it over. An owner file that cannot be read fails the killer with `killer-error` and exit code 1. The exceptions are a non-directory at the mutex path, which has no owner file and is judged by age, and an owner read that fails with `ENOENT` or `EACCES`, which counts as no owner. If releasing the mutex fails after the takeover has finished, the killer logs `killer-mutex-release-error` with the error code. The takeover result stands: the killer keeps the lock it acquired and continues supervising.\n105:After the selected runtime exits, `skilled-loop` declines unless the record is armed, fresh and below the hop limit. It consumes an eligible record, increments the hop count and launches that runtime with a seed that carries the Gate 3 answer:\n112:If the record is still pending when the runtime exits, `skilled-loop` prints the runtime-specific manual claim command and preserves the runtime's exit code. For a valid repo-relative `spec_folder`, the wrapper adds a `Spec folder:` suffix with the packet path. When the field is `none`, it adds a Skip suffix. An empty field adds no suffix, so the receiver asks Gate 3 as usual. A valid path carries the sending session's answer across the hop. AGENTS.md states that a relaunch hop is the same session. The receiver claims the note and reads its four sections. For a packet path, it runs `/speckit:resume`. For `none`, it skips resume and treats Gate 3 as answered D) Skip. It works only on the task's Remaining work. It never runs `prepare` for the claimed note. It runs `complete` when the task work ends:\n172:A prepare exit code of 2 means the note or loop cannot be prepared. Common reasons include:\n181:When prepare exits 3, start Claude through skilled-loop or use the printed manual claim command. At the hop cap, prepare writes no record, logs `prepare-declined reason=hop-cap`, and its first line reads `[session-relaunch] hop limit reached; continue in this session (auto-compaction remains the fallback)`. A corrupt relaunch JSON file is a decline with reason `no-file`. An unreadable Claude transcript yields no context usage, and unreadable Claude settings files are skipped as absent.\n183:If the killer logs `killer-no-armed-record`, it found no armed record before signalling. `killer-hop-cap` means the record's hop count already reached `SESSION_RELAUNCH_MAX_HOPS`, so the killer unarmed it with reason `hop-cap` and sent no signal. `killer-lock-held` means another killer owns the lock or is creating it. `killer-lock-stale` means the owner process is gone or the unreadable lock is older than five seconds. `killer-lock-not-owned` means the lock no longer named this killer's pid when it exited, because a stall past the expiry let a contender take it over, so the killer left the file in place for its new owner. `target-gone` marks a target that exited between the final liveness check and SIGTERM. The killer polls every 100 ms. The idle timeout defaults to 30000 ms and the separate post-signal grace period is 5000 ms. If either wait expires while Claude remains alive, the killer disarms the record and logs `killer-timeout`. A transcript that stays unreadable for 1 s also ends with `killer-timeout`. `killer-timeout-superseded` replaces it when a later prepare armed a different note during the wait. The killer then logs the `watched` and `current` note paths and neither disarms nor notifies, because the newer note still needs its own killer. A signal probe that fails with `EPERM` means the pid belongs to another user's process, so the killer treats its target as gone instead of signalling it. `killer-lock-held` also appears while another killer holds the `<lock>.takeover` mutex. If an armed record has no live killer, the next arm event starts one. A live killer that owns the lock blocks that restart by design. `killer-error` with exit code 1 means the takeover mutex's owner file could not be read, for example because the owner path is a directory. The killer stops before the takeover and the armed record stays armed for the next arm event. `killer-mutex-release-error` means the `<lock>.takeover` directory could not be removed after the takeover finished. The takeover result stands and the killer keeps the lock it acquired, so this line does not mean the handoff failed. A leftover mutex is reclaimed by the next takeover once its owner is dead, or once it has no owner file and is older than 30 s.\n185:When Claude exits with code 130 and a relaunch record is eligible, the wrapper prints the manual claim command and exits 130 without relaunching. If the saved cwd is missing or the cwd or handoff path contains CR or LF, it prints the command and exits 1. A consume failure prints a warning and the wrapper continues with the exit or relaunch it was already handling. If the wrapper cannot decode the record returned by `should-relaunch`, it prints `warning: could not read the relaunch record` and consumes the record. It exits 1 when `should-relaunch` approved the hop and exits with the runtime's own exit code when `should-relaunch` declined. Neither path prints a manual claim command. Other `should-relaunch` failures with status 2 or higher print a warning and preserve Claude's exit code.\n","metadata":{"output":"65:The wrapper exports one loop id and starts `SKILLED_LOOP_HOPS` at 0. It relaunches only when a matching record is armed, fresh and below the configured hop limit.\n73:The nudge reports the fill as `[session-relaunch] Context is about N% full.`, or as `[session-relaunch] Context is past the configured window (over 100% full).` when the ratio is above 1. It then lets the session finish instead: `If the remaining work fits in a few more steps, finish it instead and skip these steps.` A session with more work left does the handoff steps now, before any other work, so a fresh session continues before auto-compaction has to. The nudge gives the operator the last word. If the operator has told the session not to hand off, it skips the note, continuity save and prepare steps, keeps working and leaves auto-compaction as the fallback. The nudge names a handoff note under `.skilled/skills/.state/session-relaunch/handoffs/` and a prepare command. The path resolves under the repository that holds the concern, so a runtime started in a subdirectory still writes under the repo root. The note needs flat frontmatter and four non-empty sections: Goal, Done, Remaining work and In-flight state. The Remaining work section lists only unfinished task work. Note creation, continuity saving and `prepare` are handoff steps. When `spec_folder` is a packet path, Claude uses `/speckit:save`. Other runtimes send the save JSON to the [continuity writer](../../../.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/dist/continuity/generate-context.js) with `--stdin --full-auto <spec-folder>` before prepare. `spec_folder` holds the repo-relative packet path, `none` when Gate 3 was answered D) Skip or empty when Gate 3 was never answered.\n85:Pi checks usage at `message_end` and holds the nudge for `agent_end`, because the message that crosses the threshold usually carries the prepare call itself. At `agent_end` it re-checks the pending or fresh armed record and the hop limit, then sends the held text with `deliverAs: 'followUp'` and `triggerTurn: true`, so the nudge reaches the model without an