tree. The automatic hop needs the session-relaunch rows in the main checkout's file. Each of those three rows, like the three session-relaunch rows in the Claude settings file, carries the repo's hook-drift fallback: a missing adapter prints a `mk-hook-drift host=<host> event=<event> adapter=<script>` line on stderr and still returns an envelope the host accepts. Claude's fallback adds `mkHookDrift: true`. Codex omits that key, because it rejects unknown keys, and answers its Stop event with `systemMessage` instead of `hookSpecificOutput`, because it rejects that envelope on Stop.\n.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/README.md:31:Hermes runs through the enabled `repo-guards` project plugin, so the operator changes nothing in the user config. The plugin bridges three events to the Node entry points under `hermes/`. `post_api_request` records each call's `usage` per session. `pre_llm_call` runs `context-nudge.mjs` and returns the seed text on a `[session-relaunch]` first turn or the handoff text once the fill crosses the threshold, appended to the turn's user message. `on_session_end` runs `session-end-arm.mjs` and accepts a missing event name from the bridge, but rejects a supplied name other than `on_session_end`. It arms a pending record for this session and detaches the killer with the Hermes process resolved by the ancestor rule. A killer spawn failure leaves the record armed until the next session end restarts its killer. Hermes fires `on_session_end` after every message, so the plugin keeps each session's recorded usage until `on_session_finalize` or `on_session_reset` clears it. The fill counts input plus cache read and cache write tokens, never output. The window is `SESSION_RELAUNCH_WINDOW_TOKENS`, then the event's `model_context_window`. If the event omits that field, the plugin resolves the window from Hermes model metadata. The 200-thousand-token default applies only when neither source provides a window. The nudge state logs which source supplied the window.\n.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/README.md:41:`SESSION_RELAUNCH_THRESHOLD` controls when each session is nudged. Keep the threshold well above a fresh session's startup fill plus its remaining work. Codex live runs at `0.12` chained hop after hop because each new session reached about 10% on startup alone. `SESSION_RELAUNCH_MAX_HOPS` caps completed relaunches and stops the chain once that limit is reached.\n.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/README.md:89:No adapter nudges once `SKILLED_LOOP_HOPS` reaches `SESSION_RELAUNCH_MAX_HOPS`, because a record prepared there could not relaunch. Every seed handler reads the note path back out of the seed, so the claim instructions it adds name the real note.\n.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/README.md:129:| disarmed | The killer ended the record without a relaunch. A session that never went idle before the deadline, a transcript unreadable for 1 s and a session still running after the SIGTERM grace period end with reason `killer-timeout`. A record whose hop count already reached `SESSION_RELAUNCH_MAX_HOPS` ends with reason `hop-cap`, and the killer sends no signal. |\n.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/README.md:155:| `SESSION_RELAUNCH_THRESHOLD` | `0.80` | Context fill ratio that triggers the first nudge. Invalid values use `0.80`. Valid values are greater than 0 and at most 1. |\n.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/README.md:156:| `SESSION_RELAUNCH_WINDOW_TOKENS` | unset, resolved automatically | A positive value overrides Claude settings. Otherwise the resolver checks the project-local settings override, then the project settings and the settings file in the home directory for `autoCompactWindow`. If none is usable, a model name containing `[1m]` uses a one-million-token fallback. Other models use a 200-thousand-token fallback. |\n.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/README.md:157:| `SESSION_RELAUNCH_MAX_HOPS` | `10` | Highest completed hop count allowed. The cap is enforced at three points: the wrapper declines a record at or above it, `prepare` at that count writes no record, prints its hop-limit line with the manual claim command and exits 3, and the killer unarms the record with reason `hop-cap` and signals nothing. No adapter nudges at the cap. Zero is accepted and declines every hop. Unset, empty, negative, non-integer or non-numeric values use 10. |\n.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/README.md:183:If the killer logs `killer-no-armed-record`, it found no armed record before signalling. `killer-hop-cap` means the record's hop count already reached `SESSION_RELAUNCH_MAX_HOPS`, so the killer unarmed it with reason `hop-cap` and sent no signal. `killer-lock-held` means another killer owns the lock or is creating it. `killer-lock-stale` means the owner process is gone or the unreadable lock is older than five seconds. `killer-lock-not-owned` means the lock no longer named this killer's pid when it exited, because a stall past the expiry let a contender take it over, so the killer left the file in place for its new owner. `target-gone` marks a target that exited between the final liveness check and SIGTERM. The killer polls every 100 ms. The idle timeout defaults to 30000 ms and the separate post-signal grace period is 5000 ms. If either wait expires while Claude remains alive, the killer disarms the record and logs `killer-timeout`. A transcript that stays unreadable for 1 s also ends with `killer-timeout`. `killer-timeout-superseded` replaces it when a later prepare armed a different note during the wait. The killer then logs the `watched` and `current` note paths and neither disarms nor notifies, because the newer note still needs its own killer. A signal probe that fails with `EPERM` means the pid belongs to another user's process, so the killer treats its target as gone instead of signalling it. `killer-lock-held` also appears while another killer holds the `<lock>.takeover` mutex. If an armed record has no live killer, the next arm event starts one. A live killer that owns the lock blocks that restart by design. `killer-error` with exit code 1 means the takeover mutex's owner file could not be read, for example because the owner path is a directory. The killer stops before the takeover and the armed record stays armed for the next arm event. `killer-mutex-release-error` means the `<lock>.takeover` directory could not be removed after the takeover finished. The takeover result stands and the killer keeps the lock it acquired, so this line does not mean the handoff failed. A leftover mutex is reclaimed by the next takeover once its owner is dead, or once it has no owner file and is older than 30 s.\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"grep -n \"autoCompactWindow\" -r .claude opencode.json .skilled 2>/dev/null | head -20; echo \"---\"; grep -rn \"SESSION_RELAUNCH_THRESHOLD\\|SESSION_RELAUNCH_WINDOW_TOKENS\\|SESSION_RELAUNCH_MAX_HOPS\" .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/README.md | head -20","time":{"start":1792430079432,"end":1792430111447}},"id":"prt_155287ca2001zu0iw9AhlIjrpw","sessionID":"ses_eaada65b7ffeHiWlwBKhGghsv7","messageID":"msg_15528414c001omSiTH2fVDVyLg"}}
