{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1792430527806,"sessionID":"ses_eaada65b7ffeHiWlwBKhGghsv7","part":{"id":"prt_1552f553b001vTN1K9nyPpuOiG","messageID":"msg_1552f4b41001mQH07X0ITtxgjs","sessionID":"ses_eaada65b7ffeHiWlwBKhGghsv7","snapshot":"a444ac98e9877ccc1d05c18ec0638cf812b7be9e","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1792430529123,"sessionID":"ses_eaada65b7ffeHiWlwBKhGghsv7","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_00_yUpwBYKWZjKiIWVZW87j0791","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/148-session-relaunch-deep-review/.skilled/commands/speckit/handoff.md"},"output":"<path>/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/148-session-relaunch-deep-review/.skilled/commands/speckit/handoff.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: ---\n2: description: \"Write the handoff note and prepare the session relaunch loop: the manual trigger on every runtime.\"\n3: argument-hint: \"(no arguments)\"\n4: allowed-tools: Read, Write, Bash\n5: ---\n6: \n7: # /speckit:handoff\n8: \n9: Manual entry point for the session relaunch loop. Writes this session's handoff note and prepares a pending record. Automatic runtimes arm it at their end event. Command-only runtimes leave it pending and use a manual claim command.\n10: \n11: ---\n12: \n13: ## 1. PURPOSE\n14: \n15: The relaunch loop carries work into a fresh session near the end of this session's useful context. This session writes a standalone handoff note. The wrapper relaunches the runtime with a claim seed. The new session picks up the remaining work. This command is the manual trigger on runtimes wired with the session-relaunch hooks and the only trigger on runtimes without a hook. Section 5 covers a session seeded with `[session-relaunch] claim and continue handoff <path>`.\n16: \n17: ---\n18: \n19: ## 2. CONTRACT\n20: \n21: **Inputs:** nothing through `$ARGUMENTS`. Both `prepare` and `claim` require `--session <id>`. Only Claude Code can omit it and use `$CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID`. On Claude, `CLAUDE_PID` supplies the runtime process id for the shell-child check. Other runtimes must pass the session id. If a runtime has no session-id variable, such as Devin or Cursor, run `uuidgen` once and reuse that id for the session. `spec_folder` records the Gate 3 answer: the repo-relative packet path, `none` after D) Skip or empty when Gate 3 was never answered.\n22: \n23: **Outputs:**\n24: \n25: - A handoff note at `.skilled/skills/.state/session-relaunch/handoffs/<UTC yyyymmddThhmmssZ>-<first 8 chars of the session id>.md`.\n26: - One `STATUS=` line per the exit code in Step 5.\n27: - When a spec packet is bound: save continuity before the note is written. Claude runs `/speckit:save`. Other runtimes pipe the save JSON to the continuity writer.\n28: \n29: **CLI:** `node .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/bin/session-relaunch.cjs`\n30: \n31: | Invocation | Effect | Exit |\n32: |---|---|---|\n33: | `prepare --handoff <path> --session <id> [--runtime <name>]` | Validate the note and write the pending relaunch record | `0` prepared (pending until an automatic arm event, or manual on command-only runtimes), `2` refused (reason on stderr, including a runtime conflict), `3` no automatic hop: not under `skilled-loop`, or the hop limit is reached |\n34: | `claim <path> --session <id>` | Move the note `open -> claimed` and print its body | `0` ok, `2` refused |\n35: | `complete <path>` | Move the note `claimed -> done` | `0` ok, `2` refused |\n36: \n37: ---\n38: \n39: ## 3. WORKFLOW OVERVIEW\n40: \n41: | Step | Name | Purpose | Output |\n42: |---|---|---|---|\n43: | 1 | Resolve inputs | Session id, cwd, spec binding | Values for the note |\n44: | 2 | Save the packet | Claude runs `/speckit:save`. Other runtimes use the continuity writer when a packet is bound | Fresh continuity |\n45: | 3 | Write the note | `status: open` handoff file | The note path |\n46: | 4 | Prepare | CLI validates and records | Exit code `0`, `2` or `3` |\n47: | 5 | Report | Translate the exit code | `STATUS=` line |\n48: \n49: ---\n50: \n51: ## 4. INSTRUCTIONS\n52: \n53: ### Step 1: Resolve inputs\n54: \n55: - Session id: both commands require `--session <id>`. Claude Code can omit the flag because the CLI reads `$CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID`. Other runtimes pass the id directly. If a runtime exposes no session-id variable, such as Devin or Cursor, run `uuidgen` once and reuse that value for the rest of the session. The id fills the note's `session_id`, the filename suffix and `--session`.\n56: - cwd: the absolute working directory.\n57: - Spec binding: use the repo-relative packet path when one is bound. Use `none` when Gate 3 was answered D) Skip. Leave the field empty when Gate 3 was never answered.\n58: \n59: ### Step 2: Save the bound packet\n60: \n61: When `spec_folder` is a packet path, save its continuity before writing the note so the receiving session's `/speckit:resume` lands on fresh state. Claude runs `/speckit:save`. Other runtimes pipe the save JSON to `node .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/dist/continuity/generate-context.js --stdin --full-auto <spec-folder>`. Record the repo-relative packet path in `spec_folder`. When Gate 3 was answered D) Skip, set `spec_folder` to `none` and skip the save. When Gate 3 was never answered, leave `spec_folder` empty.\n62: \n63: ### Step 3: Write the handoff note\n64: \n65: Create the directory first:\n66: \n67: ```bash\n68: mkdir -p .skilled/skills/.state/session-relaunch/handoffs\n69: ```\n70: \n71: Name the file `<UTC yyyymmddThhmmssZ>-<first 8 chars of session id>.md`. `date -u +%Y%m%dT%H%M%SZ` produces the stamp and `${SESSION_ID:0:8}` the suffix. The note format is exact:\n72: \n73: ```markdown\n74: ---\n75: status: open\n76: session_id: <id of the session that wrote it>\n77: loop_id: <SKILLED_LOOP_ID or empty>\n78: runtime: <runtime-name>\n79: cwd: <absolute cwd>\n80: spec_folder: <repo-relative packet path, none if Gate 3 was answered D) Skip or empty if Gate 3 was never answered>\n81: created_at: <ISO-8601 UTC>\n82: ---\n83: ## Goal\n84: <non-empty>\n85: ## Done\n86: <non-empty>\n87: ## Remaining work\n88: <non-empty>\n89: ## In-flight state\n90: <non-empty>\n91: ```\n92: \n93: Frontmatter is flat `key: value` lines only: no nesting, no lists. `loop_id` is `$SKILLED_LOOP_ID`, empty when the session is not running under the wrapper. `runtime` is the runtime that wrote the note. Every section must be non-empty. Goal states what the work is for. Done records finished work. Remaining work lists the claimable tail. In-flight state preserves the active work the next session must not lose, including open files, half-applied edits, running processes and pending decisions.\n94: \n95: ### Step 4: Prepare\n96: \n97: ```bash\n98: node .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/bin/session-relaunch.cjs prepare --handoff <path> --session <session id> --runtime <runtime>\n99: ```\n100: \n101: Pass `--session <id>` to `prepare`. Only Claude Code can omit it and use `CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID`. The `--runtime` value must be one of `claude`, `pi`, `opencode`, `codex`, `cursor`, `hermes` or `devin`. Outside the loop, it overrides the environment or Claude default. Inside the loop, it must match `SKILLED_LOOP_RUNTIME` or prepare exits 2. The handoff nudge passes the session id and runtime.\n102: \n103: ### Step 5: Report by exit code\n104: \n105: - `0`: prepared. The record is pending until the runtime's arm event fires. Command-only runtimes leave it pending for the wrapper's manual command. Report `STATUS=OK RESULT=prepared HANDOFF=<path>` and stop.\n106: - `2`: refused. Show the stderr reason verbatim. Fix the cause it names, then run `prepare` again. The cause may be an invalid note, a note owned by another session or a live shell child of the runtime process. Report `STATUS=FAIL ERROR=\"<reason>\"` only when the refusal cannot be repaired.\n107: - `3`: no automatic hop. Either the session is not running under `skilled-loop`, or the loop has reached its hop limit, in which case the CLI first prints `hop limit reached; continue in this session` and auto-compaction stays the fallback. Both cases print the claim command for the selected runtime. Show it verbatim and report `STATUS=OK RESULT=manual HANDOFF=<path>`.\n108: \n109: | Runtime | Printed command |\n110: |---|---|\n111: | Claude | `skilled-loop claude \"[session-relaunch] claim and continue handoff <path>\"` |\n112: | Pi | `skilled-loop pi \"[session-relaunch] claim and continue handoff <path>\"` |\n113: | OpenCode | `skilled-loop opencode --prompt \"[session-relaunch] claim and continue handoff <path>\"` |\n114: | Codex | `skilled-loop codex \"[session-relaunch] claim and continue handoff <path>\"` |\n115: | Cursor | `skilled-loop cursor \"[session-relaunch] claim and continue handoff <path>\"` |\n116: | Hermes | `skilled-loop hermes chat -q \"[session-relaunch] claim and continue handoff <path>\"` |\n117: | Devin | `skilled-loop devin -- \"[session-relaunch] claim and continue handoff <path>\"` |\n118: \n119: The CLI and wrapper place each path `!` in single quotes outside the seed's double quotes. For example, they print `skilled-loop claude \"[session-relaunch] claim and continue handoff /tmp/han\"'!'\"doff.md\"`. Interactive Bash keeps that path intact when history expansion is on.\n120: \n121: ---\n122: \n123: ## 5. RECEIVING A HANDOFF\n124: \n125: A session seeded with `[session-relaunch] claim and continue handoff <path>` is on the other side of this protocol. It:\n126: \n127: 1. Runs `node .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/bin/session-relaunch.cjs claim <path> --session <session id>`, which moves the note `open -> claimed` and prints its body. Only Claude Code may omit `--session` and use `CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID`. A runtime without a session-id variable generates an id with `uuidgen` and reuses it for the session.\n128: 2. Reads the note's Goal, Done, Remaining work and In-flight state sections.\n129: 3. Runs `/speckit:resume <spec_folder>` when the field contains a repo-relative packet path. When it is `none`, Gate 3 was answered D) Skip and the receiver does not ask it again. When it is empty, Gate 3 was never answered and the receiver asks it as usual.\n130: 4. Continues the Remaining work.\n131: 5. Runs `node .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/bin/session-relaunch.cjs complete <path>` when the handoff is fully absorbed.\n132: \n133: The note's status moves only `open -> claimed -> done`. When this session prepares a successor hop, `prepare` marks every other note this session claimed as `done`, sets `completed_at` and adds `superseded_by` with the new note's basename. It leaves open notes, done notes, notes claimed by another session and the new note unchanged.\n134: \n135: ---\n136: \n137: ## 6. EXAMPLES\n138: \n139: **Example 1: inside the loop**\n140: \n141: ```text\n142: /speckit:handoff\n143: ```\n144: \n145: The note is written and `prepare` exits `0`. On an automatic runtime, its arm event can qualify the record for relaunch when the session ends.\n146: \n147: **Example 2: outside the loop**\n148: \n149: ```text\n150: /speckit:handoff\n151: ```\n152: \n153: `prepare` exits `3` and the command shows the selected runtime's printed claim command so the operator can run the hop by hand.\n154: \n155: ---\n156: \n157: ## 7. RELATED COMMANDS\n158: \n159: - `/speckit:save`: used before the note on Claude when a spec packet is bound. Other runtimes use the continuity writer.\n160: - `/speckit:resume`: runs on the receiving side when the note names a `spec_folder`.\n\n(End of file - total 160 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"---\ndescription: \"Write the handoff note and prepare the session relaunch loop: the manual trigger on every runtime.\"\nargument-hint: \"(no arguments)\"\nallowed-tools: Read, Write, Bash\n---\n\n# /speckit:handoff\n\nManual entry point for the session relaunch loop. Writes this session's handoff note and prepares a pending record. Automatic runtimes arm it at their end event. Command-only runtimes leave it pending and use a manual claim command.\n\n---\n\n## 1. PURPOSE\n\nThe relaunch loop carries work into a fresh session near the end of this session's useful context. This session writes a standalone handoff note. The wrapper relaunches the runtime with a claim seed. The new session picks up the remaining work. This command is the manual trigger on runtimes wired with the session-relaunch hooks and the only trigger on runtimes without a hook. Section 5 covers a session seeded with `[session-relaunch] claim and continue handoff <path>`.\n\n---\n\n## 2. CONTRACT\n","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/148-session-relaunch-deep-review/.skilled/commands/speckit/handoff.md","text":"---\ndescription: \"Write the handoff note and prepare the session relaunch loop: the manual trigger on every runtime.\"\nargument-hint: \"(no arguments)\"\nallowed-tools: Read, Write, Bash\n---\n\n# /speckit:handoff\n\nManual entry point for the session relaunch loop. Writes this session's handoff note and prepares a pending record. Automatic runtimes arm it at their end event. Command-only runtimes leave it pending and use a manual claim command.\n\n---\n\n## 1. PURPOSE\n\nThe relaunch loop carries work into a fresh session near the end of this session's useful context. This session writes a standalone handoff note. The wrapper relaunches the runtime with a claim seed. The new session picks up the remaining work. This command is the manual trigger on runtimes wired with the session-relaunch hooks and the only trigger on runtimes without a hook. Section 5 covers a session seeded with `[session-relaunch] claim and continue handoff <path>`.\n\n---\n\n## 2. CONTRACT\n\n**Inputs:** nothing through `$ARGUMENTS`. Both `prepare` and `claim` require `--session <id>`. Only Claude Code can omit it and use `$CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID`. On Claude, `CLAUDE_PID` supplies the runtime process id for the shell-child check. Other runtimes must pass the session id. If a runtime has no session-id variable, such as Devin or Cursor, run `uuidgen` once and reuse that id for the session. `spec_folder` records the Gate 3 answer: the repo-relative packet path, `none` after D) Skip or empty when Gate 3 was never answered.\n\n**Outputs:**\n\n- A handoff note at `.skilled/skills/.state/session-relaunch/handoffs/<UTC yyyymmddThhmmssZ>-<first 8 chars of the session id>.md`.\n- One `STATUS=` line per the exit code in Step 5.\n- When a spec packet is bound: save continuity before the note is written. Claude runs `/speckit:save`. Other runtimes pipe the save JSON to the continuity writer.\n\n**CLI:** `node .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/bin/session-relaunch.cjs`\n\n| Invocation | Effect | Exit |\n|---|---|---|\n| `prepare --handoff <path> --session <id> [--runtime <name>]` | Validate the note and write the pending relaunch record | `0` prepared (pending until an automatic arm event, or manual on command-only runtimes), `2` refused (reason on stderr, including a runtime conflict), `3` no automatic hop: not under `skilled-loop`, or the hop limit is reached |\n| `claim <path> --session <id>` | Move the note `open -> claimed` and print its body | `0` ok, `2` refused |\n| `complete <path>` | Move the note `claimed -> done` | `0` ok, `2` refused |\n\n---\n\n## 3. WORKFLOW OVERVIEW\n\n| Step | Name | Purpose | Output |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Resolve inputs | Session id, cwd, spec binding | Values for the note |\n| 2 | Save the packet | Claude runs `/speckit:save`. Other runtimes use the continuity writer when a packet is bound | Fresh continuity |\n| 3 | Write the note | `status: open` handoff file | The note path |\n| 4 | Prepare | CLI validates and records | Exit code `0`, `2` or `3` |\n| 5 | Report | Translate the exit code | `STATUS=` line |\n\n---\n\n## 4. INSTRUCTIONS\n\n### Step 1: Resolve inputs\n\n- Session id: both commands require `--session <id>`. Claude Code can omit the flag because the CLI reads `$CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID`. Other runtimes pass the id directly. If a runtime exposes no session-id variable, such as Devin or Cursor, run `uuidgen` once and reuse that value for the rest of the session. The id fills the note's `session_id`, the filename suffix and `--session`.\n- cwd: the absolute working directory.\n- Spec binding: use the repo-relative packet path when one is bound. Use `none` when Gate 3 was answered D) Skip. Leave the field empty when Gate 3 was never answered.\n\n### Step 2: Save the bound packet\n\nWhen `spec_folder` is a packet path, save its continuity before writing the note so the receiving session's `/speckit:resume` lands on fresh state. Claude runs `/speckit:save`. Other runtimes pipe the save JSON to `node .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/dist/continuity/generate-context.js --stdin --full-auto <spec-folder>`. Record the repo-relative packet path in `spec_folder`. When Gate 3 was answered D) Skip, set `spec_folder` to `none` and skip the save. When Gate 3 was never answered, leave `spec_folder` empty.\n\n### Step 3: Write the handoff note\n\nCreate the directory first:\n\n```bash\nmkdir -p .skilled/skills/.state/session-relaunch/handoffs\n```\n\nName the file `<UTC yyyymmddThhmmssZ>-<first 8 chars of session id>.md`. `date -u +%Y%m%dT%H%M%SZ` produces the stamp and `${SESSION_ID:0:8}` the suffix. The note format is exact:\n\n```markdown\n---\nstatus: open\nsession_id: <id of the session that wrote it>\nloop_id: <SKILLED_LOOP_ID or empty>\nruntime: <runtime-name>\ncwd: <absolute cwd>\nspec_folder: <repo-relative packet path, none if Gate 3 was answered D) Skip or empty if Gate 3 was never answered>\ncreated_at: <ISO-8601 UTC>\n---\n## Goal\n<non-empty>\n## Done\n<non-empty>\n## Remaining work\n<non-empty>\n## In-flight state\n<non-empty>\n```\n\nFrontmatter is flat `key: value` lines only: no nesting, no lists. `loop_id` is `$SKILLED_LOOP_ID`, empty when the session is not running under the wrapper. `runtime` is the runtime that wrote the note. Every section must be non-empty. Goal states what the work is for. Done records finished work. Remaining work lists the claimable tail. In-flight state preserves the active work the next session must not lose, including open files, half-applied edits, running processes and pending decisions.\n\n### Step 4: Prepare\n\n```bash\nnode .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/bin/session-relaunch.cjs prepare --handoff <path> --session <session id> --runtime <runtime>\n```\n\nPass `--session <id>` to `prepare`. Only Claude Code can omit it and use `CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID`. The `--runtime` value must be one of `claude`, `pi`, `opencode`, `codex`, `cursor`, `hermes` or `devin`. Outside the loop, it overrides the environment or Claude default. Inside the loop, it must match `SKILLED_LOOP_RUNTIME` or prepare exits 2. The handoff nudge passes the session id and runtime.\n\n### Step 5: Report by exit code\n\n- `0`: prepared. The record is pending until the runtime's arm event fires. Command-only runtimes leave it pending for the wrapper's manual command. Report `STATUS=OK RESULT=prepared HANDOFF=<path>` and stop.\n- `2`: refused. Show the stderr reason verbatim. Fix the cause it names, then run `prepare` again. The cause may be an invalid note, a note owned by another session or a live shell child of the runtime process. Report `STATUS=FAIL ERROR=\"<reason>\"` only when the refusal cannot be repaired.\n- `3`: no automatic hop. Either the session is not running under `skilled-loop`, or the loop has reached its hop limit, in which case the CLI first prints `hop limit reached; continue in this session` and auto-compaction stays the fallback. Both cases print the claim command for the selected runtime. Show it verbatim and report `STATUS=OK RESULT=manual HANDOFF=<path>`.\n\n| Runtime | Printed command |\n|---|---|\n| Claude | `skilled-loop claude \"[session-relaunch] claim and continue handoff <path>\"` |\n| Pi | `skilled-loop pi \"[session-relaunch] claim and continue handoff <path>\"` |\n| OpenCode | `skilled-loop opencode --prompt \"[session-relaunch] claim and continue handoff <path>\"` |\n| Codex | `skilled-loop codex \"[session-relaunch] claim and continue handoff <path>\"` |\n| Cursor | `skilled-loop cursor \"[session-relaunch] claim and continue handoff <path>\"` |\n| Hermes | `skilled-loop hermes chat -q \"[session-relaunch] claim and continue handoff <path>\"` |\n| Devin | `skilled-loop devin -- \"[session-relaunch] claim and continue handoff <path>\"` |\n\nThe CLI and wrapper place each path `!` in single quotes outside the seed's double quotes. For example, they print `skilled-loop claude \"[session-relaunch] claim and continue handoff /tmp/han\"'!'\"doff.md\"`. Interactive Bash keeps that path intact when history expansion is on.\n\n---\n\n## 5. RECEIVING A HANDOFF\n\nA session seeded with `[session-relaunch] claim and continue handoff <path>` is on the other side of this protocol. It:\n\n1. Runs `node .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/bin/session-relaunch.cjs claim <path> --session <session id>`, which moves the note `open -> claimed` and prints its body. Only Claude Code may omit `--session` and use `CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID`. A runtime without a session-id variable generates an id with `uuidgen` and reuses it for the session.\n2. Reads the note's Goal, Done, Remaining work and In-flight state sections.\n3. Runs `/speckit:resume <spec_folder>` when the field contains a repo-relative packet path. When it is `none`, Gate 3 was answered D) Skip and the receiver does not ask it again. When it is empty, Gate 3 was never answered and the receiver asks it as usual.\n4. Continues the Remaining work.\n5. Runs `node .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/bin/session-relaunch.cjs complete <path>` when the handoff is fully absorbed.\n\nThe note's status moves only `open -> claimed -> done`. When this session prepares a successor hop, `prepare` marks every other note this session claimed as `done`, sets `completed_at` and adds `superseded_by` with the new note's basename. It leaves open notes, done notes, notes claimed by another session and the new note unchanged.\n\n---\n\n## 6. EXAMPLES\n\n**Example 1: inside the loop**\n\n```text\n/speckit:handoff\n```\n\nThe note is written and `prepare` exits `0`. On an automatic runtime, its arm event can qualify the record for relaunch when the session ends.\n\n**Example 2: outside the loop**\n\n```text\n/speckit:handoff\n```\n\n`prepare` exits `3` and the command shows the selected runtime's printed claim command so the operator can run the hop by hand.\n\n---\n\n## 7. RELATED COMMANDS\n\n- `/speckit:save`: used before the note on Claude when a spec packet is bound. Other runtimes use the continuity writer.\n- `/speckit:resume`: runs on the receiving side when the note names a `spec_folder`.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":160,"totalLines":160,"truncated":false}},"title":".skilled/commands/speckit/handoff.md","time":{"start":1792430529116,"end":1792430529121}},"id":"prt_1552f59b3001aZt06ndRZm81uc","sessionID":"ses_eaada65b7ffeHiWlwBKhGghsv7","messageID":"msg_1552f4b41001mQH07X0ITtxgjs"}}
