{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1792430550674,"sessionID":"ses_eaada65b7ffeHiWlwBKhGghsv7","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_00_7vyBDtVLdBKe6WsgHk1t5937","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"grep -n \"relaunch\\|runtime state\\|session-relaunch\" AGENTS.md | head -20; echo \"---007 SPEC---\"; grep -n \"exempt\\|state/\\|record\" specs/hooks/023-session-relaunch-loop/007-handoff-gate-exemptions/spec.md | head -30"},"output":"70:- **Artifact trigger — binds on what you are about to write, independently of the advisor score.** Before the FIRST code write, route through `sk-code`. Before the FIRST `.md` write, route through `sk-doc`, except spec-folder docs, which are `system-spec-kit`'s, and gitignored runtime state under `.skilled/skills/.state/`, such as a session-relaunch handoff note, which no skill owns. Routing means loading what the router resolves, under the same loading rule as Gate 5. A skill already in context is not re-read. A resolved contract that is wrong for this case is followed and amended, as PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK step 4 says.\n75:**Fires when** the turn will write a file — creating, editing, deleting, moving, or generating one — or will write continuity state (a save, a resume, a further iteration). **Does not fire** when the request is purely read-only: review, audit, inspect, analyze, explain, standing alone, or when its only write is gitignored runtime state under `.skilled/skills/.state/`, such as a session-relaunch handoff note. A read-only word next to a write trigger does not disqualify it.\n85:- **The answer holds for the ENTIRE session.** A relaunch hop is the same session. Re-ask only when the user says \"new task\" or \"different feature\", names a different spec folder, or asks you to.\n---007 SPEC---\n2:title: \"Feature Specification: Phase 7: handoff-gate-exemptions\"\n3:description: \"A relaunch handoff note is gitignored runtime state, yet the spec gate and the framework gate rules treated its write as spec work. A session with no Gate 3 answer could not hop without an operator prompt, and the repo's OpenCode default model failed provider routing. This phase exempts that path, states the exemption in AGENTS.md and pins the default model.\"\n5:  - \"handoff gate exemptions\"\n6:  - \"phase 7 handoff gate exemptions\"\n11:# Feature Specification: Phase 7: handoff-gate-exemptions\n30:| **Handoff Criteria** | `validate.sh` prints `RESULT: PASSED` for this folder and recursively for the parent, every row in `acceptance-criteria.md` is Met and the run 10 record is in the benchmark reports. |\n38:This is **Phase 7** of the Session relaunch loop specification. It closes the two framework gaps that phase 006 recorded as G20 and G21, and it pins the OpenCode default model that phase 006 recorded as a limitation.\n40:**Scope Boundary**: The spec-gate exemption for runtime state, the two AGENTS.md sentences that decide whether a turn is spec work, the repo `opencode.json` default model and the live proof on Claude, Pi and OpenCode with no Gate 3 answer. The items that stay out are listed in section 3.\n43:- `../006-deep-review-2-remediation/`, whose live run 5 recorded G20 and G21 in `evidence/live-runs-2026-10-19/sr-pi-run5-pane.txt`.\n47:- A spec-gate exemption and a test for it that failed before the fix.\n50:- The live run 10 evidence and a benchmark run folder for it, `2026-10-19--manual-testing-playbook--session-relaunch-gate-exemptions`.\n62:A session that has no Gate 3 answer and reaches its context limit hands off by writing a note under `.skilled/skills/.state/`. In phase 006's Pi live run 5, the session applied the Gate 3 rule, which fires when a turn will write a file, and asked the operator before it wrote the note (`006-deep-review-2-remediation/evidence/live-runs-2026-10-19/sr-pi-run5-pane.txt`, lines 184 to 203). The same session then routed the note through the Gate 2 artifact trigger, and the sk-doc router answered `clarify`, so it asked the operator again (lines 307 to 334). A third problem sat in the spec-gate hook. With enforcement on and a gate open, a Write to the handoff note was denied, which would have made the hop impossible.\n75:- A spec-gate exemption for runtime state under `.skilled/skills/.state/`, except a concern's tracked `README.md`.\n76:- A regression test for that exemption, which failed before the fix.\n86:- Codex, Cursor, Devin and Hermes live reruns. The Codex result stays as phase 006 recorded it. The other three were not run in this phase.\n94:| `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/hooks/lib/spec-gate/spec-gate-core.mjs` | Modify | Adds `RUNTIME_STATE_RELATIVE_PATH` (line 71) and the exemption in `isExemptTargetPath` (lines 1573 to 1579) |\n95:| `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/tests/hooks/spec-gate-core.test.mjs` | Modify | Adds the exemption test at line 896 |\n98:| `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/benchmark/reports/2026-10-19--manual-testing-playbook--session-relaunch-gate-exemptions/` | Create | The run 10 record |\n103:| `specs/hooks/023-session-relaunch-loop/007-handoff-gate-exemptions/` | Create | This folder and its `evidence/live-run10-2026-10-19/` |\n115:| REQ-001 | The spec gate treats a write under `.skilled/skills/.state/` as exempt, which covers gitignored runtime state such as a relaunch handoff note. A concern's `README.md` directly under that folder stays gated, and a `.state` folder nested under another path stays gated. |\n122:| REQ-002 | `AGENTS.md` states the runtime-state exemption in the Gate 3 \"does not fire\" clause and in the Gate 2 artifact trigger. |\n130:Each row is one gap that phase 006 recorded and the change that closes it.\n136:| OpenCode default | The default model failed with \"Direct provider routing is not available\" | `opencode.json` pins `llmgateway/deepseek-v4.1-flash` with variant `max` (lines 3, 6 to 7 and 10 to 11) | Stored message rows carry `modelID` `deepseek-v4.1-flash` and `variant` `max`, recorded during this phase |\n145:- **SC-001**: The run 10 record shows zero operator prompts on Claude, Pi and OpenCode, and a note that reads `status: done` for each hop.\n146:- **SC-002**: `spec-gate-core.test.mjs` finishes with `fail 0` and exit 0, and it includes the exemption test.\n157:| Risk | The exemption leaves `.state` writes ungated | Low | The exemption is path-scoped. Concern READMEs and nested `.state` paths stay gated, and the test holds both. `.gitignore` line 132 keeps the handoff notes out of commits |\n158:| Risk | The home `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` copy lacks the exemption, and Claude loads both files | Med | The home copy waits for the operator. Until then, the repo file carries the rule |\n174:- **NFR-S01**: The exemption applies only after the path is resolved through real symlinks and `..` segments, so a symlink or a relative path cannot pass as runtime state (`spec-gate-core.mjs`, `isExemptTargetPath`).\n179:- **NFR-R02**: Run 10 recorded zero operator prompts across three runtimes, and three of three hops relaunched.\n188:- Empty input: an empty or blank path is exempt, as it was before this phase. The change does not alter that behavior.\n","metadata":{"output":"70:- **Artifact trigger — binds on what you are about to write, independently of the advisor score.** Before the FIRST code write, route through `sk-code`. Before the FIRST `.md` write, route through `sk-doc`, except spec-folder docs, which are `system-spec-kit`'s, and gitignored runtime state under `.skilled/skills/.state/`, such as a session-relaunch handoff note, which no skill owns. Routing means loading what the router resolves, under the same loading rule as Gate 5. A skill already in context is not re-read. A resolved contract that is wrong for this case is followed and amended, as PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK step 4 says.\n75:**Fires when** the turn will write a file — creating, editing, deleting, moving, or generating one — or will write continuity state (a save, a resume, a further iteration). **Does not fire** when the request is purely read-only: review, audit, inspect, analyze, explain, standing alone, or when its only write is gitignored runtime state under `.skilled/skills/.state/`, such as a session-relaunch handoff note. A read-only word next to a write trigger does not disqualify it.\n85:- **The answer holds for the ENTIRE session.** A relaunch hop is the same session. Re-ask only when the user says \"new task\" or \"different feature\", names a different spec folder, or asks you to.\n---007 SPEC---\n2:title: \"Feature Specification: Phase 7: handoff-gate-exemptions\"\n3:description: \"A relaunch handoff note is gitignored runtime state, yet the spec gate and the framework gate rules treated its write as spec work. A session with no Gate 3 answer could not hop without an operator prompt, and the repo's OpenCode default model failed provider routing. This phase exempts that path, states the exemption in AGENTS.md and pins the default model.\"\n5:  - \"handoff gate exemptions\"\n6:  - \"phase 7 handoff gate exemptions\"\n11:# Feature Specification: Phase 7: handoff-gate-exemptions\n30:| **Handoff Criteria** | `validate.sh` prints `RESULT: PASSED` for this folder and recursively for the parent, every row in `acceptance-criteria.md` is Met and the run 10 record is in the benchmark reports. |\n38:This is **Phase 7** of the Session relaunch loop specification. It closes the two framework gaps that phase 006 recorded as G20 and G21, and it pins the OpenCode default model that phase 006 recorded as a limitation.\n40:**Scope Boundary**: The spec-gate exemption for runtime state, the two AGENTS.md sentences that decide whether a turn is spec work, the repo `opencode.json` default model and the live proof on Claude, Pi and OpenCode with no Gate 3 answer. The items that stay out are listed in section 3.\n43:- `../006-deep-review-2-remediation/`, whose live run 5 recorded G20 and G21 in `evidence/live-runs-2026-10-19/sr-pi-run5-pane.txt`.\n47:- A spec-gate exemption and a test for it that failed before the fix.\n50:- The live run 10 evidence and a benchmark run folder for it, `2026-10-19--manual-testing-playbook--session-relaunch-gate-exemptions`.\n62:A session that has no Gate 3 answer and reaches its context limit hands off by writing a note under `.skilled/skills/.state/`. In phase 006's Pi live run 5, the session applied the Gate 3 rule, which fires when a turn will write a file, and asked the operator before it wrote the note (`006-deep-review-2-remediation/evidence/live-runs-2026-10-19/sr-pi-run5-pane.txt`, lines 184 to 203). The same session then routed the note through the Gate 2 artifact trigger, and the sk-doc router answered `clarify`, so it asked the operator again (lines 307 to 334). A third problem sat in the spec-gate hook. With enforcement on and a gate open, a Write to the handoff note was denied, which would have made the hop impossible.\n75:- A spec-gate exemption for runtime state under `.skilled/skills/.state/`, except a concern's tracked `README.md`.\n76:- A regression test for that exemption, which failed before the fix.\n86:- Codex, Cursor, Devin and Hermes live reruns. The Codex result stays as phase 006 recorded it. The other three were not run in this phase.\n94:| `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/hooks/lib/spec-gate/spec-gate-core.mjs` | Modify | Adds `RUNTIME_STATE_RELATIVE_PATH` (line 71) and the exemption in `isExemptTargetPath` (lines 1573 to 1579) |\n95:| `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/tests/hooks/spec-gate-core.test.mjs` | Modify | Adds the exemption test at line 896 |\n98:| `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/benchmark/reports/2026-10-19--manual-testing-playbook--session-relaunch-gate-exemptions/` | Create | The run 10 record |\n103:| `specs/hooks/023-session-relaunch-loop/007-handoff-gate-exemptions/` | Create | This folder and its `evidence/live-run10-2026-10-19/` |\n115:| REQ-001 | The spec gate treats a write under `.skilled/skills/.state/` as exempt, which covers gitignored runtime state such as a relaunch handoff note. A concern's `README.md` directly under that folder stays gated, and a `.state` folder nested under another path stays gated. |\n122:| REQ-002 | `AGENTS.md` states the runtime-state exemption in the Gate 3 \"does not fire\" clause and in the Gate 2 artifact trigger. |\n130:Each row is one gap that phase 006 recorded and the change that closes it.\n136:| OpenCode default | The default model failed with \"Direct provider routing is not available\" | `opencode.json` pins `llmgateway/deepseek-v4.1-flash` with variant `max` (lines 3, 6 to 7 and 10 to 11) | Stored message rows carry `modelID` `deepseek-v4.1-flash` and `variant` `max`, recorded during this phase |\n145:- **SC-001**: The run 10 record shows zero operator prompts on Claude, Pi and OpenCode, and a note that reads `status: done` for each hop.\n146:- **SC-002**: `spec-gate-core.test.mjs` finishes with `fail 0` and exit 0, and it includes the exemption test.\n157:| Risk | The exemption leaves `.state` writes ungated | Low | The exemption is path-scoped. Concern READMEs and nested `.state` paths stay gated, and the test holds both. `.gitignore` line 132 keeps the handoff notes out of commits |\n158:| Risk | The home `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` copy lacks the exemption, and Claude loads both files | Med | The home copy waits for the operator. Until then, the repo file carries the rule |\n174:- **NFR-S01**: The exemption applies only after the path is resolved through real symlinks and `..` segments, so a symlink or a relative path cannot pass as runtime state (`spec-gate-core.mjs`, `isExemptTargetPath`).\n179:- **NFR-R02**: Run 10 recorded zero operator prompts across three runtimes, and three of three hops relaunched.\n188:- Empty input: an empty or blank path is exempt, as it was before this phase. The change does not alter that behavior.\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"grep -n \"relaunch\\|runtime state\\|session-relaunch\" AGENTS.md | head -20; echo \"---007 SPEC---\"; grep -n \"exempt\\|state/\\|record\" specs/hooks/023-session-relaunch-loop/007-handoff-gate-exemptions/spec.md | head -30","time":{"start":1792430550630,"end":1792430550653}},"id":"prt_1552fad56001ndFHOenhR5tOuU","sessionID":"ses_eaada65b7ffeHiWlwBKhGghsv7","messageID":"msg_1552f85bd001BH36cS3OIfvZxV"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1792430550897,"sessionID":"ses_eaada65b7ffeHiWlwBKhGghsv7","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_01_tYNeuV4m7oPfLyItOD617485","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"sed -n 120,145p .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/README.md; echo \"---PLAYBOOK---\"; sed -n 1,80p .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/manual-testing-playbook/plugins-and-hooks/session-relaunch-command-runtimes.md"},"output":"\n## 4. STATE FILES\n\nThe relaunch record uses `XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/skilled-relaunch` when `XDG_RUNTIME_DIR` is set. When it is unset, the record uses `skilled-relaunch-<uid>` under the operating system temporary directory, because a shared temp directory would otherwise let the first user on a host create a directory the second user cannot own. The directory must be a real directory owned by the current user. A symlink, a plain file or a foreign owner fails with `relaunch state directory must be a real directory owned by the current user: <dir>`, and a directory that passes is set to mode 0700. Its filename is `relaunch-<loop-id>.json`.\n\n| Relaunch status | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| pending | Prepare accepted the note. Stop has not armed the record yet. |\n| armed | A matching Stop event found no remaining background tasks and started the killer. |\n| disarmed | The killer ended the record without a relaunch. A session that never went idle before the deadline, a transcript unreadable for 1 s and a session still running after the SIGTERM grace period end with reason `killer-timeout`. A record whose hop count already reached `SESSION_RELAUNCH_MAX_HOPS` ends with reason `hop-cap`, and the killer sends no signal. |\n\nA corrupt relaunch file is treated as absent. `should-relaunch` declines it with reason `no-file` and logs `corrupt-relaunch-file`. The CLI also treats `EACCES`, `ENOTDIR` and `EISDIR` while reading the record as absent. Other errors return exit 2.\n\nThe handoff note has a separate status field:\n\n| Note status | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| open | Written and ready for a receiving session. |\n| claimed | The receiving session claimed it. The note records `claimed_by` and `claimed_at`. |\n| done | The receiving session completed the handoff, or `prepare` closed a claimed note when this session prepared a successor hop. |\n\nWhen a new hop goes ahead, `prepare` marks every other note this session claimed as `done`, adds `completed_at` and sets `superseded_by` to the new note's basename.\n\nThe hook keeps per-session nudge levels in `nudge-<session-id>.json` in the same runtime state directory. The hooks and wrapper append events to `relaunch.log` on a best-effort basis. The killer appends its events there too. A failed append in the killer is not swallowed. Each event is one line, appended under fsync at mode 0600, with the mode re-pinned on every append. When the previous last line has no newline, as after a partial write, the new event starts on its own line, so concurrent writers keep one event per line. A short write, from a full disk or a file-size limit, throws `relaunch log short write: wrote X of Y bytes to <path>` instead of losing the event quietly. The killer lock file stores the owner pid, the creation timestamp and the expiry stamp. A lock past its expiry counts as stale, so a reused pid cannot keep the loop pinned. Active or newly created locks log `killer-lock-held`. Stale locks log `killer-lock-stale` before the killer removes and retries them.\n\n---\n---PLAYBOOK---\n---\ntitle: \"Codex, Cursor, Hermes and Devin Session Relaunch\"\ndescription: \"Automatable and live scenarios for the Codex and Hermes session-relaunch adapters and the Cursor and Devin command-trigger runtimes.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"Codex session relaunch\"\n  - \"Cursor session relaunch\"\n  - \"Hermes session relaunch\"\n  - \"Devin session relaunch\"\n  - \"session-relaunch-command-runtimes\"\n  - \"skilled-loop codex\"\n  - \"skilled-loop hermes\"\nversion: 1.0.3.0\nid: session-relaunch-command-runtimes\nexpected_workflow_mode: UNKNOWN\nexpected_leaf_resources: []\n---\n\n# Codex, Cursor, Hermes and Devin Session Relaunch\n\n---\n\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\nThis scenario covers Codex and Hermes automatic adapters and the Cursor and Devin `/speckit:handoff` command-trigger paths. Automatable checks cover the Codex and Hermes adapter suites, the shared nudge suite and the stub-runtime wrapper suite. Live tmux checks observe a handoff through each runtime.\n\n### Why This Matters\n\nEach runtime uses a different seed argument form, and only two of the four have an automatic arm event. A live run confirms the host delivers the events each adapter needs and the restarted runtime claims the prepared handoff, or that the command-trigger path gives the operator a working manual hop.\n\n---\n\n## 2. SCENARIO CONTRACT\n\nThis scenario defines four automatable checks and one live check per runtime. The recorded-results table below captures the operator outcomes from 2026-10-18. A live check marked SKIP was omitted by operator decision.\n\n- Objective: Verify Codex and Hermes adapter loading, event handling, seed injection and matching record arming, and verify the Cursor and Devin wrapper arms plus the `/speckit:handoff` command-trigger hop.\n- Real user request: Carry a short task through a context handoff in Codex or Hermes, or through the handoff command in Cursor or Devin, then finish it in the new session.\n- Prompt: `Carry this short task through a context handoff, then finish it in the new session. Tell me when you are ready.`\n- Expected execution process: Run the Codex and Hermes adapter suites, the shared nudge suite and the stub-runtime wrapper suite. Then start each runtime in its own tmux session with a low hop cap and follow the generated handoff. The nudge's prepare command includes `--runtime <name>`. On Cursor and Devin, run `/speckit:handoff` and start the hop with the selected runtime's claim command printed by the CLI.\n- Expected signals: Automatable commands exit 0. The stub-runtime suite records `codex --no-daemon -m <model> <seed>`, `cursor-agent --model <model> <seed>`, `hermes chat --provider <p> -m <model> -q <seed>` and `devin --model <model> -- <seed>` on relaunch, with valid model and provider values carried on every hop and no resume-family flag passed. Model, name, Pi extension and Pi or Hermes provider scans leave values equal to `--` or starting with `-` in the first launch's arguments and leave them out of relaunch arguments. Each live automatic run arms its matching record and restarts with the claim seed. Each command-trigger run produces a note, a pending record and a runtime-specific manual claim command. If a runtime exits with a record still pending, the wrapper prints its runtime-specific manual command, consumes the record and preserves the runtime exit code. If it cannot decode the record returned by `should-relaunch`, it prints `warning: could not read the relaunch record` and consumes the record. On an approved hop the wrapper relaunches the runtime with the seed. It exits 1 for an unreadable or empty record, which prints no manual claim command. It also exits 1 for an empty cwd, a line break in the cwd or handoff path, or a failed `cd`, and prints the manual claim command in those cases. A runtime exit of 130 prints the manual claim command and exits 130. On a decline the wrapper exits with the runtime's own exit code and prints the manual claim command only for the `not-armed`, `stale` and `hop-cap` reasons. Keep `SESSION_RELAUNCH_THRESHOLD` well above a fresh session's startup fill plus its remaining work. Codex live runs chained at `0.12` because each fresh session reached about 10% on startup alone. `SESSION_RELAUNCH_MAX_HOPS` caps completed relaunches.\n- Desired user-visible outcome: The receiving session claims the same handoff and continues the unfinished task.\n- Pass/fail: PASS if all four automatable checks pass and each live run that was run shows the matching runtime restart and claim seed, or the command-trigger hop delivering the claim seed by hand. Record an operator-directed SKIP for a live check that was not run. FAIL if a smoke or suite exits non-zero, the wrapper uses the wrong runtime arguments or a live run does not reach the expected handoff.\n\n---\n\n## 3. TEST EXECUTION\n\n### Primary Scenario\n\n| Feature ID | Feature Name | Scenario Objective | Exact Prompt | Exact Command Sequence | Expected Signals | Evidence | Pass/Fail Criteria | Failure Triage |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| session-relaunch-command-runtimes | Codex, Cursor, Hermes and Devin session relaunch | Run four automatable checks and one live check per runtime. | Carry this short task through a context handoff, then finish it in the new session. Tell me when you are ready. | Codex adapter suite -> Hermes adapter suite -> shared nudge suite -> stub-runtime wrapper suite -> live Codex tmux hop -> live Hermes tmux hop -> live Cursor command hop -> live Devin command hop | Automatable commands exit 0. The stub suite records the four relaunch argv forms with valid model and provider values carried and no resume flags. Codex and Hermes live runs arm the matching record and restart with the claim seed. Cursor and Devin produce a note, a pending record and a printed runtime-specific manual command. | Capture command output and exit codes. For each live run capture the tmux transcript, runtime log entries and handoff status from open through claimed. | PASS when every automated check exits 0 and each live check reaches the claim seed by its runtime's trigger. FAIL when an automated check fails, a runtime is mismatched or a live claim seed is not delivered. | 1. Confirm the adapter path and host smoke command. 2. Check adapter event logs and the matching runtime field in the pending record. 3. Check the killer result, hop limit and runtime-specific seed arguments. 4. On command-only runtimes, confirm prepare printed the manual claim command. |\n\n### Automatable Checks\n\n#### Codex adapter suite (Automatable)\n\n~~~bash\nnode --test .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/tests/codex-adapters.test.cjs\n~~~\n\nEvidence: the suite covers the `UserPromptSubmit` seed path with the carried spec folder, the once-at-threshold and threshold-plus-0.05 nudges on `PostToolUse`, the nudge that stays silent while this session already has a prepared handoff or at the hop limit, unique adapter registration per event, `Stop` arming with the rollout transcript and the kill switch. PASS when every case passes.\n\n#### Hermes adapter suite (Automatable)\n\n~~~bash\nnode --test .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/tests/hermes-adapters.test.cjs\n~~~\n\nEvidence: the suite covers the captured payload fixtures, the Hermes usage fill that counts input and cache tokens and never output, the seed first turn, threshold levels, window-source resolution, `on_session_end` arming with the ancestor pid lookup, pending restore on a failed killer spawn and the kill switch. PASS when every case passes.\n\n#### Shared nudge suite (Automatable)\n\n~~~bash\nnode --test .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/tests/nudge.test.cjs\n~~~\n\nEvidence: the suite covers the shared handoff and seed text, the level rule and `specFolderFromSeed` parsing used by every new adapter. PASS when every case passes.\n\n#### Stub-runtime wrapper arms (Automatable)\n","metadata":{"output":"\n## 4. STATE FILES\n\nThe relaunch record uses `XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/skilled-relaunch` when `XDG_RUNTIME_DIR` is set. When it is unset, the record uses `skilled-relaunch-<uid>` under the operating system temporary directory, because a shared temp directory would otherwise let the first user on a host create a directory the second user cannot own. The directory must be a real directory owned by the current user. A symlink, a plain file or a foreign owner fails with `relaunch state directory must be a real directory owned by the current user: <dir>`, and a directory that passes is set to mode 0700. Its filename is `relaunch-<loop-id>.json`.\n\n| Relaunch status | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| pending | Prepare accepted the note. Stop has not armed the record yet. |\n| armed | A matching Stop event found no remaining background tasks and started the killer. |\n| disarmed | The killer ended the record without a relaunch. A session that never went idle before the deadline, a transcript unreadable for 1 s and a session still running after the SIGTERM grace period end with reason `killer-timeout`. A record whose hop count already reached `SESSION_RELAUNCH_MAX_HOPS` ends with reason `hop-cap`, and the killer sends no signal. |\n\nA corrupt relaunch file is treated as absent. `should-relaunch` declines it with reason `no-file` and logs `corrupt-relaunch-file`. The CLI also treats `EACCES`, `ENOTDIR` and `EISDIR` while reading the record as absent. Other errors return exit 2.\n\nThe handoff note has a separate status field:\n\n| Note status | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| open | Written and ready for a receiving session. |\n| claimed | The receiving session claimed it. The note records `claimed_by` and `claimed_at`. |\n| done | The receiving session completed the handoff, or `prepare` closed a claimed note when this session prepared a successor hop. |\n\nWhen a new hop goes ahead, `prepare` marks every other note this session claimed as `done`, adds `completed_at` and sets `superseded_by` to the new note's basename.\n\nThe hook keeps per-session nudge levels in `nudge-<session-id>.json` in the same runtime state directory. The hooks and wrapper append events to `relaunch.log` on a best-effort basis. The killer appends its events there too. A failed append in the killer is not swallowed. Each event is one line, appended under fsync at mode 0600, with the mode re-pinned on every append. When the previous last line has no newline, as after a partial write, the new event starts on its own line, so concurrent writers keep one event per line. A short write, from a full disk or a file-size limit, throws `relaunch log short write: wrote X of Y bytes to <path>` instead of losing the event quietly. The killer lock file stores the owner pid, the creation timestamp and the expiry stamp. A lock past its expiry counts as stale, so a reused pid cannot keep the loop pinned. Active or newly created locks log `killer-lock-held`. Stale locks log `killer-lock-stale` before the killer removes and retries them.\n\n---\n---PLAYBOOK---\n---\ntitle: \"Codex, Cursor, Hermes and Devin Session Relaunch\"\ndescription: \"Automatable and live scenarios for the Codex and Hermes session-relaunch adapters and the Cursor and Devin command-trigger runtimes.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"Codex session relaunch\"\n  - \"Cursor session relaunch\"\n  - \"Hermes session relaunch\"\n  - \"Devin session relaunch\"\n  - \"session-relaunch-command-runtimes\"\n  - \"skilled-loop codex\"\n  - \"skilled-loop hermes\"\nversion: 1.0.3.0\nid: session-relaunch-command-runtimes\nexpected_workflow_mode: UNKNOWN\nexpected_leaf_resources: []\n---\n\n# Codex, Cursor, Hermes and Devin Session Relaunch\n\n---\n\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\nThis scenario covers Codex and Hermes automatic adapters and the Cursor and Devin `/speckit:handoff` command-trigger paths. Automatable checks cover the Codex and Hermes adapter suites, the shared nudge suite and the stub-runtime wrapper suite. Live tmux checks observe a handoff through each runtime.\n\n### Why This Matters\n\nEach runtime uses a different seed argument form, and only two of the four have an automatic arm event. A live run confirms the host delivers the events each adapter needs and the restarted runtime claims the prepared handoff, or that the command-trigger path gives the operator a working manual hop.\n\n---\n\n## 2. SCENARIO CONTRACT\n\nThis scenario defines four automatable checks and one live check per runtime. The recorded-results table below captures the operator outcomes from 2026-10-18. A live check marked SKIP was omitted by operator decision.\n\n- Objective: Verify Codex and Hermes adapter loading, event handling, seed injection and matching record arming, and verify the Cursor and Devin wrapper arms plus the `/speckit:handoff` command-trigger hop.\n- Real user request: Carry a short task through a context handoff in Codex or Hermes, or through the handoff command in Cursor or Devin, then finish it in the new session.\n- Prompt: `Carry this short task through a context handoff, then finish it in the new session. Tell me when you are ready.`\n- Expected execution process: Run the Codex and Hermes adapter suites, the shared nudge suite and the stub-runtime wrapper suite. Then start each runtime in its own tmux session with a low hop cap and follow the generated handoff. The nudge's prepare command includes `--runtime <name>`. On Cursor and Devin, run `/speckit:handoff` and start the hop with the selected runtime's claim command printed by the CLI.\n- Expected signals: Automatable commands exit 0. The stub-runtime suite records `codex --no-daemon -m <model> <seed>`, `cursor-agent --model <model> <seed>`, `hermes chat --provider <p> -m <model> -q <seed>` and `devin --model <model> -- <seed>` on relaunch, with valid model and provider values carried on every hop and no resume-family flag passed. Model, name, Pi extension and Pi or Hermes provider scans leave values equal to `--` or starting with `-` in the first launch's arguments and leave them out of relaunch arguments. Each live automatic run arms its matching record and restarts with the claim seed. Each command-trigger run produces a note, a pending record and a runtime-specific manual claim command. If a runtime exits with a record still pending, the wrapper prints its runtime-specific manual command, consumes the record and preserves the runtime exit code. If it cannot decode the record returned by `should-relaunch`, it prints `warning: could not read the relaunch record` and consumes the record. On an approved hop the wrapper relaunches the runtime with the seed. It exits 1 for an unreadable or empty record, which prints no manual claim command. It also exits 1 for an empty cwd, a line break in the cwd or handoff path, or a failed `cd`, and prints the manual claim command in those cases. A runtime exit of 130 prints the manual claim command and exits 130. On a decline the wrapper exits with the runtime's own exit code and prints the manual claim command only for the `not-armed`, `stale` and `hop-cap` reasons. Keep `SESSION_RELAUNCH_THRESHOLD` well above a fresh session's startup fill plus its remaining work. Codex live runs chained at `0.12` because each fresh session reached about 10% on startup alone. `SESSION_RELAUNCH_MAX_HOPS` caps completed relaunches.\n- Desired user-visible outcome: The receiving session claims the same handoff and continues the unfinished task.\n- Pass/fail: PASS if all four automatable checks pass and each live run that was run shows the matching runtime restart and claim seed, or the command-trigger hop delivering the claim seed by hand. Record an operator-directed SKIP for a live check that was not run. FAIL if a smoke or suite exits non-zero, the wrapper uses the wrong runtime arguments or a live run does not reach the expected handoff.\n\n---\n\n## 3. TEST EXECUTION\n\n### Primary Scenario\n\n| Feature ID | Feature Name | Scenario Objective | Exact Prompt | Exact Command Sequence | Expected Signals | Evidence | Pass/Fail Criteria | Failure Triage |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| session-relaunch-command-runtimes | Codex, Cursor, Hermes and Devin session relaunch | Run four automatable checks and one live check per runtime. | Carry this short task through a context handoff, then finish it in the new session. Tell me when you are ready. | Codex adapter suite -> Hermes adapter suite -> shared nudge suite -> stub-runtime wrapper suite -> live Codex tmux hop -> live Hermes tmux hop -> live Cursor command hop -> live Devin command hop | Automatable commands exit 0. The stub suite records the four relaunch argv forms with valid model and provider values carried and no resume flags. Codex and Hermes live runs arm the matching record and restart with the claim seed. Cursor and Devin produce a note, a pending record and a printed runtime-specific manual command. | Capture command output and exit codes. For each live run capture the tmux transcript, runtime log entries and handoff status from open through claimed. | PASS when every automated check exits 0 and each live check reaches the claim seed by its runtime's trigger. FAIL when an automated check fails, a runtime is mismatched or a live claim seed is not delivered. | 1. Confirm the adapter path and host smoke command. 2. Check adapter event logs and the matching runtime field in the pending record. 3. Check the killer result, hop limit and runtime-specific seed arguments. 4. On command-only runtimes, confirm prepare printed the manual claim command. |\n\n### Automatable Checks\n\n#### Codex adapter suite (Automatable)\n\n~~~bash\nnode --test .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/tests/codex-adapters.test.cjs\n~~~\n\nEvidence: the suite covers the `UserPromptSubmit` seed path with the carried spec folder, the once-at-threshold and threshold-plus-0.05 nudges on `PostToolUse`, the nudge that stays silent while this session already has a prepared handoff or at the hop limit, unique adapter registration per event, `Stop` arming with the rollout transcript and the kill switch. PASS when every case passes.\n\n#### Hermes adapter suite (Automatable)\n\n~~~bash\nnode --test .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/tests/hermes-adapters.test.cjs\n~~~\n\nEvidence: the suite covers the captured payload fixtures, the Hermes usage fill that counts input and cache tokens and never output, the seed first turn, threshold levels, window-source resolution, `on_session_end` arming with the ancestor pid lookup, pending restore on a failed killer spawn and the kill switch. PASS when every case passes.\n\n#### Shared nudge suite (Automatable)\n\n~~~bash\nnode --test .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/tests/nudge.test.cjs\n~~~\n\nEvidence: the suite covers the shared handoff and seed text, the level rule and `specFolderFromSeed` parsing used by every new adapter. PASS when every case passes.\n\n#### Stub-runtime wrapper arms (Automatable)\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"sed -n 120,145p .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/README.md; echo \"---PLAYBOOK---\"; sed -n 1,80p .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/manual-testing-playbook/plugins-and-hooks/session-relaunch-command-runtimes.md","time":{"start":1792430550871,"end":1792430550894}},"id":"prt_1552fae6f001LNtBC6TqZX4Wp6","sessionID":"ses_eaada65b7ffeHiWlwBKhGghsv7","messageID":"msg_1552f85bd001BH36cS3OIfvZxV"}}
