printed by earlier builds spell the Skip suffix differently. The receiver still reads them as Skip. AGENTS.md states that a relaunch hop is the same session. The receiving runtime claims the note and continues only its task work. It never runs prepare on an already claimed note.\n\n`SESSION_RELAUNCH_THRESHOLD` controls when each session is nudged. Keep it well above a fresh session's startup fill plus its remaining work. Codex live runs chained hop after hop at `0.12` because each fresh session reached about 10% on startup alone. `SESSION_RELAUNCH_MAX_HOPS` caps completed relaunches. At the cap the chain ends on its own: prepare writes no record, the adapters stop nudging and the killer un-arms an armed record, so a re-nudged receiver is never killed for a relaunch that cannot start. Use a low hop cap for live checks.\n\n### Pi And OpenCode Adapters\n\nPi checks usage at message_end and holds the nudge text there rather than sending it, because the message that crosses the threshold usually carries the prepare call itself. agent_end re-checks the record and the hop cap, then delivers the held text with deliverAs: 'followUp', triggerTurn: true, so the nudge starts its own turn and needs no operator input. It captures a matching seed in before_agent_start and arms the Pi record on agent_end. Its killer-path resolver walks up from the current directory, so it can find killer.cjs from a repository subdirectory. tsconfig.pi.json includes the Pi extension entry for type-checking.\n\nOpenCode reads message.updated and message.part.updated. It pushes the active nudge on every request while this session has no handoff. Outside skilled-loop it keeps pushing until the handoff note exists. A matching claim seed stays in the system prompt on every request of the session until the note leaves the open state or a new non-seed user message arrives, because an auxiliary request can consume the first prompt. The seed enters through experimental.chat.system.transform. The relaunch record is armed on session.idle.\n\nThe wrapper's Pi relaunch carries provider and model selection together with -ne and each -e extension selection from the initial launch. In this repository, start Pi with -ne -e .pi/extensions/session-relaunch.ts because two installed extensions both register --fast. OpenCode is started with -m <provider/model>. The wrapper preserves that model selection across hops.\n\nIn the recorded live runs, OpenCode reported about 1M context tokens for openai/gpt-6-luna and Pi reported 272K. These reported window sizes differ by runtime.\n\n### Codex Adapter\n\nCodex binds three repo hooks rendered from hook-registry.json into .codex/hooks.json, one script per event because the Codex hook installer allows each script under one event only. `prompt-seed.mjs` on UserPromptSubmit returns claim-seed instructions for a `[session-relaunch]` prompt and runs the threshold check for any other prompt. `context-nudge.mjs` on PostToolUse runs the same check mid-session. `stop-arm.mjs` accepts only `hook_event_name: Stop`. It arms a matching pending record before spawning the killer with `--transcript <transcript_path>`, so a spawn failure leaves the record armed until the next Stop restarts its killer.\n\nThe fill reads the rollout's latest `token_count` event at `transcript_path`. The numerator is `last_token_usage.input_tokens`, which already contains the cached share, so `cached_input_tokens` is not added. The window is `SESSION_RELAUNCH_WINDOW_TOKENS`, then the event's `model_context_window`. The nudge reaches the model as `hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext`. Codex exports no pid variable, so the killer target resolves through the ancestor rule the CLI's running-work check uses.\n\n### Hermes Adapter\n\nHermes runs through the enabled `repo-guards` project plugin, which bridges its plugin hooks to the Node entry points under `hermes/`. `post_api_request` records each call's `usage` per session. `pre_llm_call` returns the context adapter's seed text on a `[session-relaunch]` first turn or the handoff text once the fill crosses the threshold, appended to the turn's user message. `on_session_end` accepts a missing event name from the plugin bridge and rejects a supplied name other than `on_session_end`. It arms a pending record for this session before detaching the killer with the Hermes process resolved by the ancestor rule, so a spawn failure leaves the record armed until the next session end restarts its killer.\n\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"grep -n \"Stop event\\|pending |\\|armed |\" .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/README.md | head -10; echo \"---CATALOG---\"; sed -n 1,80p .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/feature-catalog/context-preservation/session-relaunch-loop.md","time":{"start":1792430578961,"end":1792430578979}},"id":"prt_155301c0c0017efwHUZ2qvsXoj","sessionID":"ses_eaada65b7ffeHiWlwBKhGghsv7","messageID":"msg_1552ffb2f001scLFnhykTD92ir"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1792430579226,"sessionID":"ses_eaada65b7ffeHiWlwBKhGghsv7","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_01_nJFNyAe3rF4Lcu8hgtoS6439","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"grep -n \"open notes\\|claimed note\\|supersede\" .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/README.md .skilled/commands/speckit/handoff.md | head -20"},"output":".skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/README.md:112:If the record is still pending when the runtime exits, `skilled-loop` prints the runtime-specific manual claim command and preserves the runtime's exit code. For a valid repo-relative `spec_folder`, the wrapper adds a `Spec folder:` suffix with the packet path. When the field is `none`, it adds a Skip suffix. An empty field adds no suffix, so the receiver asks Gate 3 as usual. A valid path carries the sending session's answer across the hop. AGENTS.md states that a relaunch hop is the same session. The receiver claims the note and reads its four sections. For a packet path, it runs `/speckit:resume`. For `none`, it skips resume and treats Gate 3 as answered D) Skip. It works only on the task's Remaining work. It never runs `prepare` for the claimed note. It runs `complete` when the task work ends:\n.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/README.md:139:| done | The receiving session completed the handoff, or `prepare` closed a claimed note when this session prepared a successor hop. |\n.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/README.md:141:When a new hop goes ahead, `prepare` marks every other note this session claimed as `done`, adds `completed_at` and sets `superseded_by` to the new note's basename.\n.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/README.md:183:If the killer logs `killer-no-armed-record`, it found no armed record before signalling. `killer-hop-cap` means the record's hop count already reached `SESSION_RELAUNCH_MAX_HOPS`, so the killer unarmed it with reason `hop-cap` and sent no signal. `killer-lock-held` means another killer owns the lock or is creating it. `killer-lock-stale` means the owner process is gone or the unreadable lock is older than five seconds. `killer-lock-not-owned` means the lock no longer named this killer's pid when it exited, because a stall past the expiry let a contender take it over, so the killer left the file in place for its new owner. `target-gone` marks a target that exited between the final liveness check and SIGTERM. The killer polls every 100 ms. The idle timeout defaults to 30000 ms and the separate post-signal grace period is 5000 ms. If either wait expires while Claude remains alive, the killer disarms the record and logs `killer-timeout`. A transcript that stays unreadable for 1 s also ends with `killer-timeout`. `killer-timeout-superseded` replaces it when a later prepare armed a different note during the wait. The killer then logs the `watched` and `current` note paths and neither disarms nor notifies, because the newer note still needs its own killer. A signal probe that fails with `EPERM` means the pid belongs to another user's process, so the killer treats its target as gone instead of signalling it. `killer-lock-held` also appears while another killer holds the `<lock>.takeover` mutex. If an armed record has no live killer, the next arm event starts one. A live killer that owns the lock blocks that restart by design. `killer-error` with exit code 1 means the takeover mutex's owner file could not be read, for example because the owner path is a directory. The killer stops before the takeover and the armed record stays armed for the next arm event. `killer-mutex-release-error` means the `<lock>.takeover` directory could not be removed after the takeover finished. The takeover result stands and the killer keeps the lock it acquired, so this line does not mean the handoff failed. A leftover mutex is reclaimed by the next takeover once its owner is dead, or once it has no owner file and is older than 30 s.\n.skilled/commands/speckit/handoff.md:133:The note's status moves only `open -> claimed -> done`. When this session prepares a successor hop, `prepare` marks every other note this session claimed as `done`, sets `completed_at` and adds `superseded_by` with the new note's basename. It leaves open notes, done notes, notes claimed by another session and the new note unchanged.\n","metadata":{"output":".skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/README.md:112:If the record is still pending when the runtime exits, `skilled-loop` prints the runtime-specific manual claim command and preserves the runtime's exit code. For a valid repo-relative `spec_folder`, the wrapper adds a `Spec folder:` suffix with the packet path. When the field is `none`, it adds a Skip suffix. An empty field adds no suffix, so the receiver asks Gate 3 as usual. A valid path carries the sending session's answer across the hop. AGENTS.md states that a relaunch hop is the same session. The receiver claims the note and reads its four sections. For a packet path, it runs `/speckit:resume`. For `none`, it skips resume and treats Gate 3 as answered D) Skip. It works only on the task's Remaining work. It never runs `prepare` for the claimed note. It runs `complete` when the task work ends:\n.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/README.md:139:| done | The receiving session completed the handoff, or `prepare` closed a claimed note when this session prepared a successor hop. |\n.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/README.md:141:When a new hop goes ahead, `prepare` marks every other note this session claimed as `done`, adds `completed_at` and sets `superseded_by` to the new note's basename.\n.skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/README.md:183:If the killer logs `killer-no-armed-record`, it found no armed record before signalling. `killer-hop-cap` means the record's hop count already reached `SESSION_RELAUNCH_MAX_HOPS`, so the killer unarmed it with reason `hop-cap` and sent no signal. `killer-lock-held` means another killer owns the lock or is creating it. `killer-lock-stale` means the owner process is gone or the unreadable lock is older than five seconds. `killer-lock-not-owned` means the lock no longer named this killer's pid when it exited, because a stall past the expiry let a contender take it over, so the killer left the file in place for its new owner. `target-gone` marks a target that exited between the final liveness check and SIGTERM. The killer polls every 100 ms. The idle timeout defaults to 30000 ms and the separate post-signal grace period is 5000 ms. If either wait expires while Claude remains alive, the killer disarms the record and logs `killer-timeout`. A transcript that stays unreadable for 1 s also ends with `killer-timeout`. `killer-timeout-superseded` replaces it when a later prepare armed a different note during the wait. The killer then logs the `watched` and `current` note paths and neither disarms nor notifies, because the newer note still needs its own killer. A signal probe that fails with `EPERM` means the pid belongs to another user's process, so the killer treats its target as gone instead of signalling it. `killer-lock-held` also appears while another killer holds the `<lock>.takeover` mutex. If an armed record has no live killer, the next arm event starts one. A live killer that owns the lock blocks that restart by design. `killer-error` with exit code 1 means the takeover mutex's owner file could not be read, for example because the owner path is a directory. The killer stops before the takeover and the armed record stays armed for the next arm event. `killer-mutex-release-error` means the `<lock>.takeover` directory could not be removed after the takeover finished. The takeover result stands and the killer keeps the lock it acquired, so this line does not mean the handoff failed. A leftover mutex is reclaimed by the next takeover once its owner is dead, or once it has no owner file and is older than 30 s.\n.skilled/commands/speckit/handoff.md:133:The note's status moves only `open -> claimed -> done`. When this session prepares a successor hop, `prepare` marks every other note this session claimed as `done`, sets `completed_at` and adds `superseded_by` with the new note's basename. It leaves open notes, done notes, notes claimed by another session and the new note unchanged.\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"grep -n \"open notes\\|claimed note\\|supersede\" .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/README.md .skilled/commands/speckit/handoff.md | head -20","time":{"start":1792430579208,"end":1792430579222}},"id":"prt_155301d3c001797OMkQxFjwGhQ","sessionID":"ses_eaada65b7ffeHiWlwBKhGghsv7","messageID":"msg_1552ffb2f001scLFnhykTD92ir"}}
