{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1792430762180,"sessionID":"ses_eaada65b7ffeHiWlwBKhGghsv7","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_00_DXpiPKjkt7DbKqJZnLj74494","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"sed -n 1,120p .skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-readme/references/readme/quality-and-checklist.md"},"output":"---\ntitle: README Quality and Pre-Publish Checklist\ndescription: DQI weighting, per-section quality checks and the full pre-publish checklist for README documents.\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"readme quality criteria\"\n  - \"readme dqi\"\n  - \"readme pre-publish checklist\"\n  - \"readme section quality checks\"\n  - \"readme review checklist\"\nimportance_tier: normal\ncontextType: implementation\nversion: 1.2.0.0\n---\n\n# README Quality and Pre-Publish Checklist\n\nOverflow detail behind `SKILL.md` Section 8 (validation and audit). Use it to review a drafted README before delivery. `SKILL.md` lists the short pre-publish checks; this file carries the DQI weighting, per-section checks and the full itemized checklist.\n\n---\n\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\nQuality for a README is weighted toward structure and content, then style. Score against the components below, then walk the pre-publish checklist for the sections the README actually includes.\n\n**Core Principle**: Apply every check only to the sections that fit the README's audience and purpose. Do not force sections a code folder never needed.\n\n**When to Use**:\n- Scoring a drafted README before delivery\n- Confirming an included section meets its quality bar\n- Running the final pre-publish pass\n\n---\n\n## 2. DQI COMPONENTS\n\n| Component | Weight | What It Measures |\n|-----------|--------|------------------|\n| **Structure** | 40% | Section organization, heading hierarchy, dividers |\n| **Content** | 35% | Commands tested, expected outputs shown, examples provided, completeness |\n| **Style** | 25% | HVR compliance, consistent formatting, code blocks with language tags |\n\n---\n\n## 3. QUALITY CHECKS FOR INCLUDED SECTIONS\n\nApply these checks only to sections that fit the README audience and purpose.\n\n| Section | Requirements |\n|---------|-------------|\n| **Overview** | 2-3 sentence description, at least one table (statistics, comparison or features) |\n| **Quick Start** | Numbered steps, copy-paste commands, verification command |\n| **Features** | Grouped by category, usage examples for key features |\n| **Structure** | ASCII tree, key files table |\n| **Configuration** | File path, all options table, environment variables |\n| **Usage Examples** | 3+ examples (simple to advanced), common patterns table |\n| **Troubleshooting** | 3+ issues, \"What you see / Causes / Fix\" format, diagnostic commands |\n| **FAQ** | 2+ questions per category, bold Q:/A: format |\n| **Related Documents** | Internal links table, external links table |\n\n---\n\n## 4. PRE-PUBLISH CHECKLIST\n\n### Structure\n\n- [ ] Title with one-line blockquote tagline for normal READMEs. Code-folder READMEs use the code-folder scaffold and do not require the tagline.\n- [ ] No Table of Contents and no `<!-- ANCHOR -->` navigation comments\n- [ ] All included sections have content (no empty sections)\n- [ ] Section numbers are sequential and H2 headings are ALL CAPS. These general format rules bind code-folder READMEs through the cross-reference in `SKILL.md` §6.\n- [ ] Section headings name the feature or artifact they introduce, not a generic label like \"Powered by\"\n- [ ] Horizontal rules between H2 sections\n- [ ] A fenced Directory Tree is present when the target folder has immediate subdirectories. A flat folder instead has a complete `CONTENTS`, `FILES` or `KEY FILES` table naming every direct file other than the README.\n\n### Content\n\n- [ ] All `[PLACEHOLDER]` markers replaced with actual content\n- [ ] Overview explains what AND why\n- [ ] Quick Start achievable in under 2 minutes\n- [ ] All commands tested and working\n- [ ] Expected outputs shown for verification commands\n- [ ] At least 3 usage examples (simple to advanced)\n- [ ] At least 3 troubleshooting entries\n- [ ] Every item in an inventory (files, templates, commands) carries a short description\n- [ ] Paragraphs run 2-4 sentences. Passages with several distinct points use bullets\n\n### Quality\n\n- [ ] All code blocks specify language\n- [ ] All internal links verified working\n- [ ] Tables properly formatted\n- [ ] No spelling or grammar errors\n- [ ] Consistent terminology throughout\n\n### Style\n\n- [ ] H2 headings use numbered ALL CAPS format\n- [ ] Numbered H3/H4 subsections use ALL CAPS (for example, `### 3.1 HYBRID SEARCH`)\n- [ ] Unnumbered H3/H4 use Title Case (for example, `### Configuration`)\n- [ ] File paths in backticks\n- [ ] Commands in fenced code blocks\n- [ ] Key terms bolded consistently\n- [ ] Emoji use matches the register: decorative section emoji only on the repository front-page README, semantic emoji (✅ ❌ ⚠️ 🔒 🚨) sparingly elsewhere\n\n### HVR Compliance\n\n- [ ] No em dashes. Use commas, periods or colons instead.\n- [ ] No semicolons. Split into two sentences or use \"and\".\n- [ ] No Oxford commas. Remove comma before final \"and/or\" in lists.\n- [ ] No \"not just X, but also Y\" patterns\n- [ ] No exactly three-item inline lists. Use 2, 4 or 5 items (tables and bullet lists exempt).\n- [ ] No setup language (\"Let's explore\", \"dive in\", \"when it comes to\")\n- [ ] No banned words (see [hvr-rules.md](../../../sk-create-with-human-voice/references/hvr-rules.md) Section 6)\n- [ ] No banned phrases (see [hvr-rules.md](../../../sk-create-with-human-voice/references/hvr-rules.md) Section 7)\n- [ ] Active voice throughout. Direct address. Simple words.\n- [ ] \"However\" used max 2 times per file\n- [ ] Max 1 ellipsis per file\n- [ ] Max 2 \"Think of it as/like\" per document\n\n---\n\n","metadata":{"output":"---\ntitle: README Quality and Pre-Publish Checklist\ndescription: DQI weighting, per-section quality checks and the full pre-publish checklist for README documents.\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"readme quality criteria\"\n  - \"readme dqi\"\n  - \"readme pre-publish checklist\"\n  - \"readme section quality checks\"\n  - \"readme review checklist\"\nimportance_tier: normal\ncontextType: implementation\nversion: 1.2.0.0\n---\n\n# README Quality and Pre-Publish Checklist\n\nOverflow detail behind `SKILL.md` Section 8 (validation and audit). Use it to review a drafted README before delivery. `SKILL.md` lists the short pre-publish checks; this file carries the DQI weighting, per-section checks and the full itemized checklist.\n\n---\n\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\nQuality for a README is weighted toward structure and content, then style. Score against the components below, then walk the pre-publish checklist for the sections the README actually includes.\n\n**Core Principle**: Apply every check only to the sections that fit the README's audience and purpose. Do not force sections a code folder never needed.\n\n**When to Use**:\n- Scoring a drafted README before delivery\n- Confirming an included section meets its quality bar\n- Running the final pre-publish pass\n\n---\n\n## 2. DQI COMPONENTS\n\n| Component | Weight | What It Measures |\n|-----------|--------|------------------|\n| **Structure** | 40% | Section organization, heading hierarchy, dividers |\n| **Content** | 35% | Commands tested, expected outputs shown, examples provided, completeness |\n| **Style** | 25% | HVR compliance, consistent formatting, code blocks with language tags |\n\n---\n\n## 3. QUALITY CHECKS FOR INCLUDED SECTIONS\n\nApply these checks only to sections that fit the README audience and purpose.\n\n| Section | Requirements |\n|---------|-------------|\n| **Overview** | 2-3 sentence description, at least one table (statistics, comparison or features) |\n| **Quick Start** | Numbered steps, copy-paste commands, verification command |\n| **Features** | Grouped by category, usage examples for key features |\n| **Structure** | ASCII tree, key files table |\n| **Configuration** | File path, all options table, environment variables |\n| **Usage Examples** | 3+ examples (simple to advanced), common patterns table |\n| **Troubleshooting** | 3+ issues, \"What you see / Causes / Fix\" format, diagnostic commands |\n| **FAQ** | 2+ questions per category, bold Q:/A: format |\n| **Related Documents** | Internal links table, external links table |\n\n---\n\n## 4. PRE-PUBLISH CHECKLIST\n\n### Structure\n\n- [ ] Title with one-line blockquote tagline for normal READMEs. Code-folder READMEs use the code-folder scaffold and do not require the tagline.\n- [ ] No Table of Contents and no `<!-- ANCHOR -->` navigation comments\n- [ ] All included sections have content (no empty sections)\n- [ ] Section numbers are sequential and H2 headings are ALL CAPS. These general format rules bind code-folder READMEs through the cross-reference in `SKILL.md` §6.\n- [ ] Section headings name the feature or artifact they introduce, not a generic label like \"Powered by\"\n- [ ] Horizontal rules between H2 sections\n- [ ] A fenced Directory Tree is present when the target folder has immediate subdirectories. A flat folder instead has a complete `CONTENTS`, `FILES` or `KEY FILES` table naming every direct file other than the README.\n\n### Content\n\n- [ ] All `[PLACEHOLDER]` markers replaced with actual content\n- [ ] Overview explains what AND why\n- [ ] Quick Start achievable in under 2 minutes\n- [ ] All commands tested and working\n- [ ] Expected outputs shown for verification commands\n- [ ] At least 3 usage examples (simple to advanced)\n- [ ] At least 3 troubleshooting entries\n- [ ] Every item in an inventory (files, templates, commands) carries a short description\n- [ ] Paragraphs run 2-4 sentences. Passages with several distinct points use bullets\n\n### Quality\n\n- [ ] All code blocks specify language\n- [ ] All internal links verified working\n- [ ] Tables properly formatted\n- [ ] No spelling or grammar errors\n- [ ] Consistent terminology throughout\n\n### Style\n\n- [ ] H2 headings use numbered ALL CAPS format\n- [ ] Numbered H3/H4 subsections use ALL CAPS (for example, `### 3.1 HYBRID SEARCH`)\n- [ ] Unnumbered H3/H4 use Title Case (for example, `### Configuration`)\n- [ ] File paths in backticks\n- [ ] Commands in fenced code blocks\n- [ ] Key terms bolded consistently\n- [ ] Emoji use matches the register: decorative section emoji only on the repository front-page README, semantic emoji (✅ ❌ ⚠️ 🔒 🚨) sparingly elsewhere\n\n### HVR Compliance\n\n- [ ] No em dashes. Use commas, periods or colons instead.\n- [ ] No semicolons. Split into two sentences or use \"and\".\n- [ ] No Oxford commas. Remove comma before final \"and/or\" in lists.\n- [ ] No \"not just X, but also Y\" patterns\n- [ ] No exactly three-item inline lists. Use 2, 4 or 5 items (tables and bullet lists exempt).\n- [ ] No setup language (\"Let's explore\", \"dive in\", \"when it comes to\")\n- [ ] No banned words (see [hvr-rules.md](../../../sk-create-with-human-voice/references/hvr-rules.md) Section 6)\n- [ ] No banned phrases (see [hvr-rules.md](../../../sk-create-with-human-voice/references/hvr-rules.md) Section 7)\n- [ ] Active voice throughout. Direct address. Simple words.\n- [ ] \"However\" used max 2 times per file\n- [ ] Max 1 ellipsis per file\n- [ ] Max 2 \"Think of it as/like\" per document\n\n---\n\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"sed -n 1,120p .skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-readme/references/readme/quality-and-checklist.md","time":{"start":1792430762163,"end":1792430762175}},"id":"prt_15532e82a001bEVB4v0CLMf5l0","sessionID":"ses_eaada65b7ffeHiWlwBKhGghsv7","messageID":"msg_15532c8b3001SuWW7jeL5fgUZ0"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1792430762322,"sessionID":"ses_eaada65b7ffeHiWlwBKhGghsv7","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_01_gLoLKuZbvi9SsrqfHFb01954","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"sed -n 1,90p .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/typescript-checklist.md"},"output":"---\ntitle: TypeScript Code Quality Checklist\ndescription: Quality validation checklist for TypeScript code in the OpenCode development environment.\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"opencode typescript checklist\"\n  - \"typescript quality validation checklist\"\n  - \"no any public api\"\n  - \"pascalcase types check\"\nimportance_tier: normal\ncontextType: implementation\nversion: 1.0.0.21\n---\n\n# TypeScript Code Quality Checklist\n\nQuality validation checklist for TypeScript code in the OpenCode development environment.\n\n---\n\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\n### Purpose\n\nSpecific quality checks for TypeScript files. Use alongside the [universal-checklist.md](./universal-checklist.md) for complete validation.\n\n### Usage\n\nApply these checks to TypeScript changes after completing the universal checklist, then use the priority levels below to determine which findings block completion.\n\n### Priority Levels\n\n| Level | Meaning       | Enforcement                        |\n|-------|---------------|------------------------------------|\n| P0    | HARD BLOCKER  | Must fix before commit             |\n| P1    | Required      | Must fix or get explicit approval  |\n| P2    | Recommended   | Can defer with justification       |\n\n---\n\n## 2. P0 - HARD BLOCKERS\n\nThese items MUST be fixed before any commit.\n\n### File Header\n\n```markdown\n[ ] File has TypeScript module header identifying the module\n```\n\n**Required format**:\n```typescript\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// MODULE: [Module Name]\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n```\n\n### No `any` in Public API\n\n```markdown\n[ ] No `any` type used in exported functions, interfaces, or type aliases\n```\n\n**Correct**:\n```typescript\nexport function parse(input: unknown): SearchConfig { }\nexport interface SearchOptions { metadata: Record<string, unknown>; }\n```\n\n**Wrong**:\n```typescript\nexport function parse(input: any): any { }\nexport interface SearchOptions { metadata: any; }\n```\n\n**Exception**: Interop with untyped third-party modules requires justification comment:\n```typescript\n// Interop: sqlite-vec has no type definitions\n// eslint-disable-next-line @typescript-eslint/no-explicit-any\nconst sqliteVec = require('sqlite-vec') as any;\n```\n\n### PascalCase Types and Interfaces\n\n```markdown\n[ ] All interfaces, type aliases, and enums use PascalCase naming\n```\n\n**Correct**:\n```typescript\ninterface SearchResult { }\n","metadata":{"output":"---\ntitle: TypeScript Code Quality Checklist\ndescription: Quality validation checklist for TypeScript code in the OpenCode development environment.\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"opencode typescript checklist\"\n  - \"typescript quality validation checklist\"\n  - \"no any public api\"\n  - \"pascalcase types check\"\nimportance_tier: normal\ncontextType: implementation\nversion: 1.0.0.21\n---\n\n# TypeScript Code Quality Checklist\n\nQuality validation checklist for TypeScript code in the OpenCode development environment.\n\n---\n\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\n### Purpose\n\nSpecific quality checks for TypeScript files. Use alongside the [universal-checklist.md](./universal-checklist.md) for complete validation.\n\n### Usage\n\nApply these checks to TypeScript changes after completing the universal checklist, then use the priority levels below to determine which findings block completion.\n\n### Priority Levels\n\n| Level | Meaning       | Enforcement                        |\n|-------|---------------|------------------------------------|\n| P0    | HARD BLOCKER  | Must fix before commit             |\n| P1    | Required      | Must fix or get explicit approval  |\n| P2    | Recommended   | Can defer with justification       |\n\n---\n\n## 2. P0 - HARD BLOCKERS\n\nThese items MUST be fixed before any commit.\n\n### File Header\n\n```markdown\n[ ] File has TypeScript module header identifying the module\n```\n\n**Required format**:\n```typescript\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// MODULE: [Module Name]\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n```\n\n### No `any` in Public API\n\n```markdown\n[ ] No `any` type used in exported functions, interfaces, or type aliases\n```\n\n**Correct**:\n```typescript\nexport function parse(input: unknown): SearchConfig { }\nexport interface SearchOptions { metadata: Record<string, unknown>; }\n```\n\n**Wrong**:\n```typescript\nexport function parse(input: any): any { }\nexport interface SearchOptions { metadata: any; }\n```\n\n**Exception**: Interop with untyped third-party modules requires justification comment:\n```typescript\n// Interop: sqlite-vec has no type definitions\n// eslint-disable-next-line @typescript-eslint/no-explicit-any\nconst sqliteVec = require('sqlite-vec') as any;\n```\n\n### PascalCase Types and Interfaces\n\n```markdown\n[ ] All interfaces, type aliases, and enums use PascalCase naming\n```\n\n**Correct**:\n```typescript\ninterface SearchResult { }\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"sed -n 1,90p .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/typescript-checklist.md","time":{"start":1792430762309,"end":1792430762321}},"id":"prt_15532e8cd00121XkmnbLE2jadX","sessionID":"ses_eaada65b7ffeHiWlwBKhGghsv7","messageID":"msg_15532c8b3001SuWW7jeL5fgUZ0"}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1792430763543,"sessionID":"ses_eaada65b7ffeHiWlwBKhGghsv7","part":{"id":"prt_15532ee150012OeDCkLcSVjgFi","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"196c8555244589f9eb60f29ed7987bb93cf26615","messageID":"msg_15532c8b3001SuWW7jeL5fgUZ0","sessionID":"ses_eaada65b7ffeHiWlwBKhGghsv7","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":283537,"input":5904,"output":125,"reasoning":772,"cache":{"write":0,"read":276736}},"cost":0.0020286072}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1792430767675,"sessionID":"ses_eaada65b7ffeHiWlwBKhGghsv7","part":{"id":"prt_15532fe33001tTA03cYKJAigUV","messageID":"msg_15532f2da001L0Q6xM9b6SCejv","sessionID":"ses_eaada65b7ffeHiWlwBKhGghsv7","snapshot":"196c8555244589f9eb60f29ed7987bb93cf26615","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1792430768952,"sessionID":"ses_eaada65b7ffeHiWlwBKhGghsv7","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_00_hlreaDFL5DiDfDnKW9ui4855","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"grep -n \"^#\\|^##\\|^###\" .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/README.md | head -40; echo \"---TREE---\"; grep -n \"tree\\|TREE\\|Key files\\|KEY FILES\\|FILES\" .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/README.md | head -10"},"output":"1:# Session Relaunch\n7:## 1. OVERVIEW\n13:### Runtime adapter matrix\n27:### Automatic triggers on Codex and Hermes\n33:### Command-only runtimes: Cursor and Devin\n39:### Runtime Behavior And Limits\n49:## 2. STARTING THE LOOP\n69:## 3. HANDOFF PROTOCOL\n121:## 4. STATE FILES\n147:## 5. SWITCHES AND DEFAULTS\n170:## 6. TROUBLESHOOTING\n189:## 7. TESTS\n208:## 8. DIRECTORY TREE\n239:## 9. RELATED\n---TREE---\n29:Codex binds three repo hooks rendered from `hook-registry.json` into [.codex/hooks.json](../../../.codex/hooks.json). `prompt-seed.mjs` binds `UserPromptSubmit`: a `[session-relaunch]` prompt receives the claim-seed instructions, and a non-seed prompt runs the same threshold check as `context-nudge.mjs`, which binds `PostToolUse`. The Codex hook installer allows each script under one event only, so seed detection and the mid-session nudge are separate entry points over `shared.mjs`. The fill comes from the transcript rollout's latest `token_count` event: `last_token_usage.input_tokens` is the numerator and `cached_input_tokens` stays out because it is already inside that count. The window is `SESSION_RELAUNCH_WINDOW_TOKENS`, then the event's `model_context_window`. The nudge reaches the model as `hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext`, the envelope the repo's other Codex adapters emit. `stop-arm.mjs` accepts only payloads whose `hook_event_name` is `Stop`. It resolves the target, arms a matching pending record, then spawns the killer with `--transcript <transcript_path>`. A spawn failure leaves the record armed until the next Stop restarts its killer. Codex exports no pid variable, so the killer target resolves through the same ancestor rule the CLI's running-work check uses. `skilled-loop codex` always starts Codex with `--no-daemon` because its shared app-server daemon keeps the environment of the session that started it. In a git worktree, Codex reads its `hooks.json` from the main checkout, not the worktree. The automatic hop needs the session-relaunch rows in the main checkout's file. Each of those three rows, like the three session-relaunch rows in the Claude settings file, carries the repo's hook-drift fallback: a missing adapter prints a `mk-hook-drift host=<host> event=<event> adapter=<script>` line on stderr and still returns an envelope the host accepts. Claude's fallback adds `mkHookDrift: true`. Codex omits that key, because it rejects unknown keys, and answers its Stop event with `systemMessage` instead of `hookSpecificOutput`, because it rejects that envelope on Stop.\n121:## 4. STATE FILES\n208:## 8. DIRECTORY TREE\n","metadata":{"output":"1:# Session Relaunch\n7:## 1. OVERVIEW\n13:### Runtime adapter matrix\n27:### Automatic triggers on Codex and Hermes\n33:### Command-only runtimes: Cursor and Devin\n39:### Runtime Behavior And Limits\n49:## 2. STARTING THE LOOP\n69:## 3. HANDOFF PROTOCOL\n121:## 4. STATE FILES\n147:## 5. SWITCHES AND DEFAULTS\n170:## 6. TROUBLESHOOTING\n189:## 7. TESTS\n208:## 8. DIRECTORY TREE\n239:## 9. RELATED\n---TREE---\n29:Codex binds three repo hooks rendered from `hook-registry.json` into [.codex/hooks.json](../../../.codex/hooks.json). `prompt-seed.mjs` binds `UserPromptSubmit`: a `[session-relaunch]` prompt receives the claim-seed instructions, and a non-seed prompt runs the same threshold check as `context-nudge.mjs`, which binds `PostToolUse`. The Codex hook installer allows each script under one event only, so seed detection and the mid-session nudge are separate entry points over `shared.mjs`. The fill comes from the transcript rollout's latest `token_count` event: `last_token_usage.input_tokens` is the numerator and `cached_input_tokens` stays out because it is already inside that count. The window is `SESSION_RELAUNCH_WINDOW_TOKENS`, then the event's `model_context_window`. The nudge reaches the model as `hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext`, the envelope the repo's other Codex adapters emit. `stop-arm.mjs` accepts only payloads whose `hook_event_name` is `Stop`. It resolves the target, arms a matching pending record, then spawns the killer with `--transcript <transcript_path>`. A spawn failure leaves the record armed until the next Stop restarts its killer. Codex exports no pid variable, so the killer target resolves through the same ancestor rule the CLI's running-work check uses. `skilled-loop codex` always starts Codex with `--no-daemon` because its shared app-server daemon keeps the environment of the session that started it. In a git worktree, Codex reads its `hooks.json` from the main checkout, not the worktree. The automatic hop needs the session-relaunch rows in the main checkout's file. Each of those three rows, like the three session-relaunch rows in the Claude settings file, carries the repo's hook-drift fallback: a missing adapter prints a `mk-hook-drift host=<host> event=<event> adapter=<script>` line on stderr and still returns an envelope the host accepts. Claude's fallback adds `mkHookDrift: true`. Codex omits that key, because it rejects unknown keys, and answers its Stop event with `systemMessage` instead of `hookSpecificOutput`, because it rejects that envelope on Stop.\n121:## 4. STATE FILES\n208:## 8. DIRECTORY TREE\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"grep -n \"^#\\|^##\\|^###\" .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/README.md | head -40; echo \"---TREE---\"; grep -n \"tree\\|TREE\\|Key files\\|KEY FILES\\|FILES\" .skilled/hooks/session-relaunch/README.md | head -10","time":{"start":1792430768933,"end":1792430768951}},"id":"prt_15533025b001rA35dhjcXx8SKK","sessionID":"ses_eaada65b7ffeHiWlwBKhGghsv7","messageID":"msg_15532f2da001L0Q6xM9b6SCejv"}}
